// Runtime/SlabArena.maxon — ⭐⭐ **THE PAGE LAYER THE SLAB SITS ON.** Everything above it — size classes,
// spans, free lists — allocates in CHUNKS, and this is what a chunk comes from and goes back to: a
// bitmap-managed ARENA committed a granule at a time, a singly-linked list of them, and the
// pointer→span reverse map a `free` needs to answer *"which span owns this address?"* without a header word
// on every object.
//
// It is v1's `stdlib/Internals.maxon:204-325` and `:1020-1093` re-derived against shv2's tiers. What is
// TAKEN from v1 is the mechanism — the bit-run arithmetic, the first-fit scan, the radix split, the
// OS-allocation guard. What is NOT taken is recorded against each item below, because every one of those is
// a decision and not an oversight.
//
// ⚠ **THE REVERSE MAP IS v1's SHAPE, NOT GO's, AND THE TWO ARE NOT INTERCHANGEABLE.** Go's `mheap.arenas`
// is a TWO-level map over ARENA numbers (`arenaL1Bits`/`arenaL2Bits`, `malloc.go:279`), reaching per-page
// metadata through the `heapArena` record it finds. This is a THREE-level map over the PAGE index, reaching
// the span pointer directly. Go's is the better structure once an arena carries per-page metadata worth
// grouping; ours carries none yet, so the extra indirection would buy a level and pay for a record.
//
// ⭐⭐ **WHERE A RESERVATION IS FREE, THE ARENA IS 64 MiB OF RESERVED ADDRESS SPACE AND 64 KiB OF COMMITTED
// MEMORY — AND THOSE ARE TWO DIFFERENT NUMBERS FOR THE FIRST TIME (F1c).** v1 maps
// `osAllocPages(0x4000000)` — 64 MiB COMMITTED, because
// `osAllocPages` reserves and commits together on every lane — so porting it would have made a hello-world
// resident in 64 MiB where the bump allocator was resident in 64 KiB. That is why this file shipped a 2 MiB
// arena and named the missing op. `StdOp.osReservePages`/`StdOp.osCommitPages` are that op, so the arena is
// now Go's shape: `__slab_arena_new` RESERVES the whole 64 MiB, commits the first `SlabArenaCommitGranuleBytes`
// of it, and `__slab_arena_alloc_chunks` commits further granules as the first-fit scan reaches them. A
// program that allocates one box is resident in one granule; the other 63.9 MiB costs address space, which
// Windows does not charge and Linux does not back.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THAT SIZE IS NOT PICKED, IT IS THE REVERSE MAP'S BOTTOM LEVEL — WHICH IS WHAT THE 2 MiB ARENA COST US.**
// An L3 table describes `ArenaMapLevelEntries` pages, i.e. 64 MiB of address space, and it is itself allocated
// out of an arena. A 2 MiB arena landing in a 64 MiB region of its own therefore spent a 64 KiB table to
// describe 2 MiB of heap — **3.1%**. At 64 MiB the arena and the table describe the same span, so the cost is
// 8 chunks in 8192 (**0.1%**) when an arena starts on a 64 MiB boundary and 16 in 8192 (**0.2%**) when it
// straddles two, which is the honest range rather than a fixed figure. See `ReservedArenaChunkCount`.
//
// ⛔⛔ **AND ON wasm32-wasi EVERY WORD OF THAT IS FALSE, WHICH IS WHY THE ARENA HAS TWO GEOMETRIES.** Linear
// memory has no reservation: `memory.grow` takes the range AND backs it, so `osReservePages` IS
// `osAllocPages` there (`StdToWasm.emitOsAllocPages` is the emitter BOTH ops route to, and its commit twin
// answers the address it was handed), and the "free" 63.9 MiB is 63.9 MiB of charged linear memory.
// **MEASURED with `scripts/peak-rss.sh --repeat 3`, two compilers built from ONE tree differing in ONE
// constant, run under `vendor/wasmtime`:**
//
//   | program                        | 64 MiB arena | 512 KiB arena |
//   |--------------------------------|-------------:|--------------:|
//   | heap-touching hello-world      |    70.32 MiB |    **5.22 MiB** |
//   | 32,768 live interpolated Strings |  67.36 MiB |   **11.10 MiB** |
//
// It was not a rounding error on that lane; at hello-world size it WAS the whole program — and the second
// row is what says the small arena has not merely moved the cost, because a heap 32k objects deep still
// fits in 11 MiB. The native lanes' emitted reservation is untouched at 67108864.
//
// ⭐⭐ **GO ANSWERS THIS WITH A PER-PLATFORM CONSTANT, AND HERE ONE OF ITS ROWS IS TAKEN AND ANOTHER IS
// DELIBERATELY NOT — WHICH IS DECIDED BY THE REASON, NOT BY THE NUMBER.** `logHeapArenaBytes`
// (`vendor/go/src/runtime/malloc.go:259`) is `(6+20)` = 64 MiB on 64-bit non-Windows-non-wasm-non-iOS,
// `(2+20)` = 4 MiB on 64-bit Windows, 32-bit and iOS/arm64, and `(9+10)` = **512 KiB on wasm**. Go states
// both reasons out loud at `:238`: Windows *"because all committed memory is charged to the process, even if
// it's not touched"*, and wasm *"because some Wasm programs have very small heap, and everything in the Wasm
// linear memory is charged."*
//
// **The wasm reason transfers exactly and the Windows one does not.** What Go commits per arena is its
// `heapArena` record, whose bitmap and span array are PROPORTIONAL to the arena, so Windows' commit charge
// bites it at 64 MiB. shv2's per-arena eager commit is `SlabArenaCommitGranuleBytes`, a CONSTANT — the
// bitmap of a 64 MiB arena is 128 words, so all of this file's metadata is 1 KiB inside a single chunk
// however large the arena grows. Windows therefore charges shv2 for one granule, and copying Go's 4 MiB
// there would be a number taken without the fact that produced it.
//
// ⇒ The lane is asked as **`IrModule.hasPageReservation`**, which is the MECHANISM and not the target: what
// this file needs to know is whether a reservation is free, and a field named `isWasm` would have had to be
// re-read by every later consumer that meant something else by it. `Compiler.maxon` sets it beside
// `debugStream`, at the seam where lowering has finished and nothing has been installed yet.
//
// ⚠ **THE ARENA IS ALIGNED TO A COMMIT GRANULE (W215), AND THE ARENA-SIZED ALIGNMENT GO USES IS A DIFFERENT
// THING THAT IS STILL DELIBERATELY NOT COPIED.** `__slab_arena_new` raises the base `osReservePages` answered
// to the next `SlabArenaCommitGranuleBytes`, because the OS promises only its own page granularity and
// x64-linux's is 4 KiB — HALF a chunk, which puts the chunk grid out of phase with the reverse map's page
// grid. See that function for the whole failure and for what it measured; `arenaAlignSlackBytes` records why
// the slack it needs is free on the lanes that pay for it and zero on the lane that does not. What follows
// is about the OTHER alignment — to the ARENA's own 64 MiB — whose slack is a whole second arena.
//
// ⚠ **AND THE PRICE OF COPYING THAT ONE IS MEASURED.**
// `sysReserveAligned` (`vendor/go/src/runtime/mem.go:185`) over-reserves `size + align` and then either TRIMS
// the unaligned ends (POSIX) or — since Windows cannot release part of a reservation — releases the whole
// thing and re-reserves at the aligned address, retrying on a race. Two things stop that here, and neither is
// effort: `osReservePages` takes a SIZE and no address hint, so the Windows half of Go's routine cannot be
// spelled at all; and a trim cannot be emitted from this tier, because `osFreePages` means *release the whole
// reservation* on Windows and *unmap this sub-range* on POSIX, and a target-neutral body cannot ask for one
// and not the other. What it would buy is 0.2% → 0.1% of the map, plus `arenaBase == addr and -size`
// — a THIRD answer to the "owning arena's base" question below, and the cheapest of the three. What it would
// cost is a `size`-sized second reservation per arena, which on wasm is not address space at all but a second
// arena's worth of charged memory — a DOUBLING of the lane the geometry above exists to shrink, where the
// granule-sized alignment above costs that lane nothing at all. ⇒
// **FOLLOW-UP, NAMED: an address-hint operand on `osReservePages`** is what makes Go's routine expressible;
// until something needs the mask, a straddling arena costs one extra 64 KiB table.
//
// ⚠ **THE CHUNK IS `SlabPageBytes` — GO'S 8 KiB PAGE — AND IT IS NOT SPELLED HERE.** `SlabClasses.maxon`
// declares `SlabPageShift`/`SlabPageBytes` and `SlabClassTable.checkSlabClassTable` already asserts the two
// agree; this file reads them. v1 writes the same number a second time and says so out loud — *"the
// arena-map index math uses its own 13-bit page masks, kept as literals there — a distinct concern"* — and
// it is not a distinct concern, it is 8192 written twice, which is how a page-size change silently leaves
// the map indexing one geometry and the class table another.
//
// ⚠ **NO LOCK, AND THAT IS INHERITED RATHER THAN CHOSEN.** shv2's existing `__slab_alloc` bumps a `.data`
// cursor with a plain load/store pair and no synchronization, so the layer beneath it adding one would buy
// nothing while the layer above it stays racy. v1's SLOW PATH holds `__slab_lock` across the callers of
// these (`__slab_alloc`'s refill, every `__slab_mcentral_*`), which is why it also needs `__slab_init_done`
// and a separate `__slab_arena_init` entry point — to run the lock's initialization exactly once. ⇒
// **FOLLOW-UP, NAMED: locking the whole slow path**, allocator and arena together, at the point green
// threads can run these on more than one OS thread.
//
// ⚠ **AND THERE IS NO `__slab_init` / `__slab_init_done` HERE FOR A SECOND REASON THAT SURVIVES THE LOCK.**
// The `.data` image IS the initialization, exactly as `MmRuntime`'s slab cursor's zero means "no arena yet":
// `__slab_arena_list` starts 0, and the first `__slab_arena_alloc_chunks` reads 0, maps an arena and
// retries. There is no state that can be observed before it is established, so there is nothing to guard.
//
// ✅ **THE OWNING ARENA'S BASE WAS "THE SCAVENGER'S DEBT" AND S6 PAID IT — BY ASKING RATHER THAN STORING.**
// `__slab_arena_free_chunks` needs an arena base its caller does not hold, and the three candidates this
// note used to list were all ways of RECORDING one at the moment a chunk run is handed out: a real out-value
// on the alloc entry point, an arena level in the reverse map, or an arena-ALIGNED reservation the address
// could be masked to. `__slab_arena_of` is a fourth and it stores nothing, on the observation that decides
// between them — **the question is asked at DESTRUCTION and would have been answered at BIRTH**, and a span
// is destroyed at most once and created at least once. See that function for the full argument, and for why
// v1's hidden `.data` out-parameter is not ported.
//
// ⚠ The masked form is still the cheapest of all four and is still unreachable for the reason above: the
// header's own note on `sysReserveAligned` records what an address-hint operand on `osReservePages` would
// buy and what it would cost on the lane with no reservation.

// --- Geometry ---
//
// ⭐ **ONE NUMBER IS CHOSEN PER LANE AND EVERYTHING ELSE IS DERIVED FROM IT.** The bitmap's width, the byte
// count and the largest run a caller may ask for all follow from the chunk count. Stated the other way round
// — a picked bitmap width beside a picked size — the two would be numbers kept in agreement by hand, which is
// exactly how the 2 MiB arena came to cost 3.1% of itself in map tables. Written as a DERIVATION there is no
// second number to disagree, so what is left to assert is that the derivation LOSES NOTHING on EITHER lane —
// `checkSlabArenaGeometry`. (It is a shift and not a division because `/` at this tier answers a float, and
// the shift's exactness is the thing being asserted anyway.)

// ⭐⭐ **THE RESERVING LANE'S ARENA IS NOT PICKED EITHER — IT IS ONE BOTTOM-LEVEL MAP TABLE'S SPAN.** An L3
// table describes `ArenaMapLevelEntries` pages, so one arena is exactly one table's worth of address space
// and the map costs 0.1–0.2% of the heap it describes. Written as the identity rather than as 8192, the two
// cannot drift.
let ReservedArenaChunkCount = ArenaMapLevelEntries

// ⭐⭐ **512 KiB WHERE A RESERVATION IS AN ALLOCATION — GO'S OWN wasm ROW, AND IT BREAKS THE COUPLING ABOVE
// ON PURPOSE.** 64 chunks of 8 KiB is `logHeapArenaBytes = (9+10)` at `vendor/go/src/runtime/malloc.go:259`,
// and it is the whole of the 70.32 → 5.22 MiB collapse the header measures. The 1:1 arena↔table match is
// gone at this size — an L3 table still describes 64 MiB of address space while only 512 KiB of it is arena —
// and the naive reading of that is a 12.5% map overhead.
//
// ⭐⭐ **IT IS ~0.1% INSTEAD, AND THE REASON IS A PROPERTY OF THE LANE THAT HAS NO RESERVATION IN THE FIRST
// PLACE.** Linear memory is a SINGLE CONTIGUOUS REGION that only ever grows at the top: `memory.grow` hands
// back the old end, so arena N+1 begins exactly where arena N stops. Successive arenas are therefore
// ADJACENT, and ~128 of them fall inside one L3 table's span — the table is allocated once and shared, not
// once per arena. The 12.5% figure is what SCATTERED arenas would cost, and this lane cannot scatter them.
//
// ⛔ **SO DO NOT "FIX" THE MISMATCH BY SHRINKING `ArenaMapLevelEntries`.** That level's width is an ADDRESS
// SPLIT (`checkSlabArenaGeometry` asserts the four fields tile 48 bits); narrowing it to match this arena
// would push bits into L2 and L1 and make every native lane's map deeper to describe a cost this lane does
// not pay.
let UnreservedArenaChunkCount = 64

// An arena's chunk count. ⚠ **RANGED FROM 1 RATHER THAN LEFT A BARE `ParsedInt`, AND THAT IS LOAD-BEARING**:
// `checkArenaMapCoupling` DIVIDES by it, and a type admitting 0 makes that a throwing divide (E3057) — the
// range is where "an arena with no chunks is not a thing" is stated. The upper bound is `i64.max` and not the
// tight `ArenaMapLevelEntries`, because a range bound must be a LITERAL: spelling 8192 here would be a second
// copy of the map level, which is the duplication this whole geometry is written as a derivation to avoid.
// The tight bound is asserted instead, with its reason, in `checkArenaMapCoupling`.
typealias ArenaChunkCount = int(1 to i64.max)

// ⭐ **EVERYTHING AN ARENA'S SIZE DECIDES, CARRIED TOGETHER SO THE PIECES CANNOT BE ASKED SEPARATELY.**
// `buildSlabArenaNew` emits the reservation size and the bitmap's byte count; `buildSlabArenaAllocChunks`
// and `buildSlabArenaFreeChunks` emit the scan bound. Handed the raw chunk count instead, each would derive
// its own — and a builder deriving `bitmapWords` from one geometry while its neighbour derives the scan
// bound from another is an allocator that hands one chunk out twice, with nothing to catch it.
type SlabArenaGeometry
	// Chunks in one arena. Every other figure here is derived from it.
	export let chunkCount as ArenaChunkCount
	// The bitmap that describes them, in machine words.
	export let bitmapWords as ParsedInt
	// ⭐⭐ **THE COMMIT GRANULES IN ONE ARENA — S6's replacement for the WATERMARK, and the reason it had to
	// go.** A watermark says *"`[0, committedChunks)` is committed"*, which is only expressible while commits
	// go one way. The scavenger decommits a granule wherever its chunks happen to have gone free, so the
	// committed set stops being a prefix the moment it runs; a bitmap is the honest shape of the same fact,
	// and it costs 16 words on the reserving lane against the watermark's one.
	export let granuleCount as ParsedInt
	// The bitmap that describes THOSE, in machine words. A CEILING, so the last word may describe fewer than
	// 64 granules — the non-reserving lane has 8 — and every walk bounds itself by `granuleCount` rather than
	// by the word count for exactly that reason.
	export let commitBitmapWords as ParsedInt
	// Where the commit bitmap sits in the metadata chunk: immediately past the chunk bitmap, whose width is
	// itself derived. Carried rather than spelled, because a lane-dependent width means a lane-dependent
	// offset, and an offset computed at each of its four use sites is four chances to describe a different
	// table.
	export let commitBitmapOffset as ByteOffset
	// The arena's own span — the address range its chunk grid covers, measured from the ALIGNED base
	// `__slab_arena_new` starts it at. `__slab_arena_of` tests an address against it.
	export let bytes as ParsedInt
	// ⭐⭐ **WHAT `__slab_arena_new` ASKS `osReservePages` FOR — `bytes` PLUS THE ROOM THE ALIGN-UP NEEDS.**
	// The base the OS answers is raised to the next commit granule (see `buildSlabArenaNew` for why it must
	// be), which can move it by as much as `SlabArenaCommitGranuleBytes - 1`; the arena then runs `bytes`
	// from there. Reserving only `bytes` would put the arena's last granule OUTSIDE the reservation, and on
	// the POSIX lanes that granule's commit is a `MAP_FIXED` re-map — a silent clobber of whatever the
	// process had at that address, not a refusal. Carried rather than derived at the one call site so
	// `checkArenaGeometryForLane` can assert the two against each other.
	export let reservationBytes as ParsedInt
	// ⭐ Which lane this geometry IS, carried rather than passed alongside. `checkArenaMapCoupling` asks a
	// DIFFERENT question of each lane, and a geometry travelling beside a separate bool is a pair that can
	// arrive mismatched — a 512 KiB arena checked against the reserving lane's rule would pass the wrong
	// assertion and say nothing.
	export let hasPageReservation as bool

	// The lane's geometry, asked of the one fact that decides it — `IrModule.hasPageReservation`, which is
	// the MECHANISM (is a reservation free?) rather than the target, for the reason its own header gives.
	export static function forReservation(hasPageReservation bool) returns Self
		if hasPageReservation 'reserves'
			return Self.fromChunkCount(ReservedArenaChunkCount, hasPageReservation: hasPageReservation)
		end 'reserves'

		return Self.fromChunkCount(UnreservedArenaChunkCount, hasPageReservation: hasPageReservation)
	end 'forReservation'

	// The derivation, written once for both lanes so a new one cannot arrive with a hand-computed bitmap.
	static function fromChunkCount(chunkCount ArenaChunkCount, hasPageReservation bool) returns Self
		let bitmapWords = chunkCount shr BitmapBitsPerWordShift
		let granuleCount = chunkCount shr SlabArenaCommitGranuleChunksShift
		let commitBitmapWords = (granuleCount + BitmapBitsPerWordMask) shr BitmapBitsPerWordShift
		let commitBitmapOffset = ((ArenaBitmapOffset as ParsedInt) + bitmapWords * ArenaWordBytes) as ByteOffset
		let bytes = chunkCount * SlabPageBytes
		return Self{chunkCount: chunkCount, bitmapWords: bitmapWords, granuleCount: granuleCount, commitBitmapWords: commitBitmapWords, commitBitmapOffset: commitBitmapOffset, bytes: bytes, reservationBytes: bytes + arenaAlignSlackBytes(hasPageReservation), hasPageReservation: hasPageReservation}
	end 'fromChunkCount'
end 'SlabArenaGeometry'

// ⭐⭐ **THE ROOM AN ARENA'S ALIGN-UP NEEDS, AND WHY THE ONE LANE THAT CANNOT AFFORD IT DOES NOT NEED IT.**
// `osReservePages` answers a base aligned to whatever the LANE'S page source hands out and nothing more —
// x64-linux's is 4 KiB, HALF a chunk — so `__slab_arena_new` raises it to the next commit granule and the
// reservation has to be that much longer.
//
// ⭐ **WHERE A RESERVATION IS FREE, SO IS THE SLACK; WHERE IT IS NOT, THERE IS NOTHING TO RAISE.** This is
// `hasPageReservation` read from both sides, and it is ONE fact rather than a lane test wearing two hats:
// the lane that answers *no* answers no because its reservation IS its allocation — `memory.grow`, whose
// unit is `UnreservedLaneGrowUnitBytes` — and a base handed out in whole grow units is already
// granule-aligned, which is exactly what makes the align-up the identity there. Slack there would be 64 KiB
// of CHARGED linear memory on a 512 KiB arena (+12.5%), against the 5.22 / 11.10 MiB the header measures.
// `checkArenaGeometryForLane` asserts the arithmetic half of that premise rather than trusting it.
function arenaAlignSlackBytes(hasPageReservation bool) returns ParsedInt
	if hasPageReservation 'reserves'
		return SlabArenaCommitGranuleBytes - 1
	end 'reserves'

	return 0
end 'arenaAlignSlackBytes'

// A bitmap word is a machine word. The shift and the mask are the same fact three times over by necessity
// (a count, the shift that divides by it, the mask that takes a remainder of it), so the pair that CAN be
// derived is derived and the one that cannot is asserted — `checkSlabArenaGeometry`.
let BitmapBitsPerWord = 64
let BitmapBitsPerWordShift = 6
let BitmapBitsPerWordMask = BitmapBitsPerWord - 1

// A machine word, for the COMPILE-TIME size arithmetic here — the bitmap's byte count and each reverse-map
// level's table size. The EMITTED `base + index · 8` is not spelled in this file at all: it is
// `MmRuntime.emitWordSlotAddr`, exported at S2 precisely so a third copy of that arithmetic did not get
// written, and `SlabRuntime`'s `WordBytes` records the same split one layer up. What stays private is the
// LAYOUT constant, because a shared "8" is not a shared fact — these are strides of tables this file owns.
let ArenaWordBytes = 8

// ⭐⭐ **THE UNIT AN ARENA COMMITS IN, AND EVERY REASON FOR THE NUMBER IS A PLATFORM'S.** 64 KiB is Windows'
// ALLOCATION GRANULARITY, it is a whole multiple of every page size the four lanes use — including
// arm64-macOS's **16 KiB**, which is TWICE `SlabPageBytes` and is the constraint that decides this — and it is
// exactly what `MmRuntime`'s arena commits today, so routing the allocator through this layer changes a
// program's resident set by nothing.
//
// ⛔ **A CHUNK-GRANULARITY COMMIT WOULD BE A WRONG ANSWER ON arm64-macOS, NOT MERELY A SMALL ONE.** A chunk is
// 8 KiB; `mmap` REFUSES an address or a length that is not page-aligned, and that lane's page is 16 KiB. So
// the granule is not a batching optimization — it is what makes every committed range page-aligned on every
// lane, which is why it is asserted below rather than tuned.
let SlabArenaCommitGranuleBytes = 65536
let SlabArenaCommitGranuleChunks = SlabArenaCommitGranuleBytes shr SlabPageShift

// The two shifts the granule is REACHED by — `chunk >> chunksShift` is its granule, `granule << shift` is its
// byte offset in the arena. A width and the shift that divides by it are ONE fact written twice by necessity,
// so both are asserted against the widths above (`requireShiftMatchesWidth`) rather than trusted. They exist
// because S6's commit bitmap and its scavenger index BY GRANULE, where the watermark they replaced only ever
// compared chunk counts.
let SlabArenaCommitGranuleChunksShift = 3
let SlabArenaCommitGranuleShift = SlabPageShift + SlabArenaCommitGranuleChunksShift

// ⭐ **THE WIDEST PAGE ANY LANE HAS, WHICH IS WHAT MAKES THE GRANULE ABOVE A CONSTRAINT RATHER THAN A TASTE.**
// x64 is 4 KiB, **arm64-macOS is 16 KiB** — TWICE a chunk, which is the constraint the granule's own header
// names — and **wasm32's `memory.grow` unit is 64 KiB**, the widest of the four. Every range this file
// reserves or commits is a whole number of granules, so a granule that is not a whole number of the widest
// page is a range `mmap` REFUSES on one lane and a `memory.grow` that silently rounds up on another.
// Asserted rather than left in a comment, which is where the 16 KiB lived until the arena acquired a second
// geometry to check it against.
let LargestTargetPageBytes = 65536

// ⭐⭐ **THE UNIT THE NON-RESERVING LANE'S PAGE SOURCE HANDS OUT — wasm's `memory.grow` PAGE.** It is 64 KiB
// by the wasm specification, and `StdToWasm.emitOsAllocPages` answers `prevPages shl 16`, so EVERY base that
// lane can hand `__slab_arena_new` is already a whole multiple of it. That is the premise
// `arenaAlignSlackBytes` rests on when it gives that lane ZERO slack, and `checkArenaReservationSlack` asks
// it as arithmetic rather than trusting it.
//
// ⛔⛔ **IT IS NOT `LargestTargetPageBytes`, THOUGH THE TWO ARE THE SAME NUMBER TODAY — one name for two
// facts is how the slack check would come to FAIL OPEN.** That one is a MAXIMUM over every lane; this one is
// ONE lane's unit, and the two checks that read them want OPPOSITE divisibility: a granule must be a whole
// number of the widest page, and the grow unit must be a whole number of granules. Written as one constant
// the pair holds only while wasm HAPPENS to be the widest lane — add a lane with a wider page and
// `granuleSplitsAPage` correctly forces the granule up with it, a max-based slack test still passes, and
// wasm's unchanged 64 KiB base stops being granule-aligned on the one lane that reserves no slack to absorb
// it. `checkSlabArenaGeometry` asserts this one cannot exceed the maximum, which is what keeps a stale pair
// from surviving a lane change quietly.
let UnreservedLaneGrowUnitBytes = 65536

// ⭐ **CHUNK 0 IS THE ARENA'S OWN METADATA, so its bit is never free and it is never handed out.** That
// costs one chunk in 8192 (0.01%) and buys two things: the metadata needs no second mapping, and a returned
// chunk address is never the arena base, so the two can never be confused by a caller that holds one.
let ArenaMetadataChunks = 1

// A bitmap byte with every bit FREE. The fill takes a BYTE and broadcasts it, so the word-level `-1` this
// file used to store is spelled as the byte that produces it — named rather than a bare `0xFF`, because
// what makes it right is the polarity (a set bit means FREE), not the hex.
let ArenaBitmapAllFreeByte = 0xFF

// The metadata chunk's layout. `next` is the arena-list link — the list is prepended to, so the newest
// arena is scanned first and a fresh one serves the request that grew it without a second walk. The two
// bitmaps follow, the chunk one first at a fixed offset and the granule COMMIT one immediately past it at
// the geometry's derived `commitBitmapOffset`.
//
// ⛔⛔ **THE `committedChunks` WATERMARK THAT USED TO SIT AT +8 IS GONE (S6), AND ITS DELETION IS THE
// SCAVENGER'S PRECONDITION RATHER THAN A TIDY-UP.** One word said *"`[0, committedChunks)` is committed"*,
// which is only true while commits go one way: `__slab_arena_alloc_chunks` raised it, nothing lowered it,
// and every range it named was therefore strictly ABOVE everything the arena had ever handed out. **A
// scavenger breaks that on its first call.** It decommits a granule wherever the chunks happen to have gone
// free — in the MIDDLE of the range the watermark claims — so the committed set stops being a prefix, and
// the watermark then reports a decommitted granule as committed and the next claim inside it hands the
// caller pages Windows has made INACCESSIBLE. The bitmap is the same fact in the shape the scavenger needs;
// it costs 16 words against the watermark's one on the reserving lane, and one word on the other.
let ArenaNextOffset = 0 as ByteOffset
let ArenaBitmapOffset = 8 as ByteOffset

// --- The reverse map's geometry ---
//
// ⭐⭐ **THREE LEVELS OVER THE PAGE INDEX, AND THE SPLIT IS ARITHMETIC RATHER THAN TASTE.** An address is
// `[ L1 | L2 | L3 | page offset ]`, and `checkSlabArenaGeometry` asserts the four widths sum to
// `ArenaMapAddressBits` — so a change to any one of them fails the next compile instead of silently
// indexing past a table. The bottom field is `SlabPageShift`, READ from the class table rather than spelled:
// the L3 index IS the page index, which is the one place v1's duplicate 13 actually bites.
//
// 48 bits is the canonical user-address width shv2 must cover: x64 user mode is 47 bits and arm64 is 48, so
// the mask is a no-op on every address a program can hold and a containment guarantee on any that is not.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE RESERVING LANE'S ARENA IS SIZED TO THIS LEVEL, WHICH IS THE COUPLING THE 2 MiB ARENA BROKE.** An
// L3 table covers `ArenaMapLevelEntries` pages — 64 MiB of address space — and `ReservedArenaChunkCount` IS
// that entry count, so one arena is one table's span. v1's arena was 64 MiB too and the match was never
// written down; here it is the DERIVATION, so the two cannot drift. An arena that starts on a 64 MiB boundary
// spends one 64 KiB table (0.1% of the arena); one that straddles spends two (0.2%). The header says what an
// ALIGNED reservation would buy and what it would cost.
//
// ⚠ **THE NON-RESERVING LANE IS 1:128 INSTEAD, AND `checkSlabArenaGeometry` CHECKS THAT RATIO RATHER THAN THE
// IDENTITY.** `UnreservedArenaChunkCount` records why the cost is still ~0.1% there — successive arenas are
// ADJACENT in linear memory, so one table serves many — and what the ratio must remain for that argument to
// hold: a WHOLE number of arenas per table. An arena that did not divide the level would put one table's
// entries under two arenas' worth of straddling, which is the 12.5% the contiguity is what avoids.
let ArenaMapAddressBits = 48
let ArenaMapLevelBits = 13
let ArenaMapLevelEntries = 8192
let ArenaMapLevelMask = ArenaMapLevelEntries - 1
let ArenaMapL1Bits = 9
let ArenaMapL1Entries = 512
let ArenaMapL1Mask = ArenaMapL1Entries - 1
let ArenaMapL2Shift = SlabPageShift + ArenaMapLevelBits
let ArenaMapL1Shift = ArenaMapL2Shift + ArenaMapLevelBits

// ⭐ **THE MISS ANSWER, NAMED.** `__slab_arena_map_get` answering 0 is not a defaulted return — it is the
// OS-DIRECT SENTINEL, and the layer above reads it as *"this pointer was never registered as belonging to a
// span, so it came straight from the OS and goes straight back"*. It is spelled here so the producer (the
// three miss blocks) and the future consumer cannot disagree about which value carries that meaning.
export let ArenaMapNoSpan = 0

// --- Entry points ---

// ⭐ **THE FAMILY BAND.** `usesSlabArena` is a `__slab_arena_` PREFIX test, deliberately narrower than
// `__slab_`: `MmRuntime`'s `__slab_alloc` already lives in that wider band, and one prefix with two owners
// is how a bit ends up set by a call that has nothing to do with it.
let SlabArenaCallPrefix = b"__slab_arena_"

let SlabArenaNewName = b"__slab_arena_new"
export let SlabArenaAllocChunksName = b"__slab_arena_alloc_chunks"
export let SlabArenaFreeChunksName = b"__slab_arena_free_chunks"
export let SlabArenaOfName = b"__slab_arena_of"
export let SlabArenaScavengeName = b"__slab_arena_scavenge"
let SlabArenaMapEnsureName = b"__slab_arena_map_ensure"
export let SlabArenaMapSetName = b"__slab_arena_map_set"
export let SlabArenaMapGetName = b"__slab_arena_map_get"

// ⚠ **THE OS-ALLOCATION GUARD KEEPS v1's NAME AND SO SITS OUTSIDE THE BAND ABOVE, WHICH IS CORRECT.** It
// guards `osAllocPages` and not the arena — S2's OS-direct path is its second caller — and it is never
// named by a Maxon-tier call, so it never needs to set a usage bit. Its installation is DECLARED by
// `installSlabArena`, which installs the family as one closure; that is rule 2 of `RuntimeUsage`'s header
// (a synthesized body's calls are declared, never discovered) and the same all-or-nothing shape
// `installManagedMemoryRuntime` uses, with dead-function elimination taking the slack back.
export let SlabAssertOsAllocName = b"__slab_assert_os_alloc"

// The two `.data` words this family owns. Both `__`-prefixed, so no user name can collide and neither
// reaches a spec golden. ⭐ Their zero initializer IS the initial state, not a default: `list == 0` means
// "no arena mapped yet" and `map l1 == 0` means "nothing registered yet", and both are what the first call
// on each path tests for.
let SlabArenaListLabel = b"__slab_arena_list"
let SlabArenaMapL1Label = b"__slab_arena_map_l1"

// Does this callee belong to the arena family? The exact twin of `isClockRuntimeCallee` and
// `isParallelBoundaryRuntimeCallee` — a prefix test, implying no other runtime floor: the bodies below call
// each other and `osAllocPages`, and nothing else. Emphatically NOT `usesHeap`: the arena is BENEATH the
// heap, and a program reaching it through the allocator carries the allocator for its own reasons.
export function isSlabArenaRuntimeCallee(callee ByteArray) returns bool
	return bytesStartWith(callee, prefix: SlabArenaCallPrefix)
end 'isSlabArenaRuntimeCallee'

// The arena family's `.data` slots. The gate is asked HERE rather than at the call site, following
// `clockRuntimeGlobals`: a condition spelled at both ends is a second place for the two to disagree, and
// the loop over an empty array is exactly the no-op the absent `if` would have produced.
export function slabArenaRuntimeGlobals(usage RuntimeUsage) returns DataSectionEntryArray
	var out = DataSectionEntryArray.create()

	if usage.usesSlabArena 'arenaSlots'
		out.push(DataSectionEntry.create(String.from(SlabArenaListLabel), valueType: StdType.i64, value: 0))
		out.push(DataSectionEntry.create(String.from(SlabArenaMapL1Label), valueType: StdType.i64, value: 0))
	end 'arenaSlots'

	return out
end 'slabArenaRuntimeGlobals'

// Install the page layer into the lowered Std module — the whole family or none of it, for the reason
// `SlabAssertOsAllocName` states.
//
// ⭐ **THE GEOMETRY CHECK RUNS BEFORE THE GATE, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE.** The gate is now live — every heap
// program reaches this layer through `__slab_alloc` (S2) — so the check would in fact run on most compiles.
// It stays ahead of the gate anyway, because the compiles it must NOT skip are the heapless ones: a geometry
// that stops satisfying its own rule has to fail the first compile after the change, and a check that only
// runs when a program happens to allocate is a check that reports the breakage to whoever comes next. It is
// two dozen integer comparisons against a compile that emits megabytes; there is nothing here worth a flag,
// and a flag is what would let it stop running.
//
// ⭐ **AND IT CHECKS BOTH LANES' GEOMETRIES, NOT THIS BUILD'S.** `module.hasPageReservation` picks what gets
// EMITTED, but a wasm-only breakage found only by a wasm compile is exactly the same skipped check one axis
// over — and both geometries are compile-time constants, so checking the pair costs nothing and cannot be
// dodged by which target happens to be building.
export function installSlabArena(module StdModule, usage RuntimeUsage)
	checkSlabArenaGeometry()

	if not usage.usesSlabArena 'noArena'
		return
	end 'noArena'

	let geometry = SlabArenaGeometry.forReservation(module.hasPageReservation)

	buildSlabAssertOsAlloc(module)
	buildSlabArenaNew(module, geometry: geometry)
	buildSlabArenaAllocChunks(module, geometry: geometry)
	buildSlabArenaFreeChunks(module, geometry: geometry)
	buildSlabArenaOf(module, geometry: geometry)
	buildSlabArenaScavenge(module, geometry: geometry)
	buildSlabArenaMapEnsure(module)
	buildSlabArenaMapSet(module)
	buildSlabArenaMapGet(module)
end 'installSlabArena'

// ⭐⭐ **THE GEOMETRY IS INTERNALLY COHERENT — asserted, not trusted.** `checkSlabClassTable` beside it makes
// the same argument for the size-class ladder and states it in full: a table whose source changed shape
// produces a working-looking allocator that indexes past its own bounds. Every number below is either
// derived from another or checked against the one it must agree with; the file declares nothing that agrees
// with nothing.
//
// It panics rather than returning a verdict, for that check's reason: a broken arena geometry cannot
// produce a working allocator, so there is no caller who could sensibly continue.
function checkSlabArenaGeometry()
	requireShiftMatchesWidth("bitmap word", shift: BitmapBitsPerWordShift, width: BitmapBitsPerWord)
	requireShiftMatchesWidth("map level", shift: ArenaMapLevelBits, width: ArenaMapLevelEntries)
	requireShiftMatchesWidth("map L1", shift: ArenaMapL1Bits, width: ArenaMapL1Entries)

	// The commit granule is a whole number of chunks (so the shift that derives its chunk count loses
	// nothing). A fact about the granule alone, so it is asked once rather than once per arena geometry.
	if SlabArenaCommitGranuleChunks * SlabPageBytes != SlabArenaCommitGranuleBytes 'granuleNotWholeChunks'
		panic("slab arena: a commit granule of {SlabArenaCommitGranuleBytes} bytes is not a whole number of {SlabPageBytes}-byte chunks")
	end 'granuleNotWholeChunks'

	// The two shifts a granule is reached by. `emitCommitGranulesFor` divides a CHUNK index by the first and
	// `buildSlabArenaScavenge` multiplies a GRANULE index by the second, so a shift that stopped matching its
	// width would commit one granule and decommit another.
	requireShiftMatchesWidth("commit granule chunks", shift: SlabArenaCommitGranuleChunksShift, width: SlabArenaCommitGranuleChunks)
	requireShiftMatchesWidth("commit granule bytes", shift: SlabArenaCommitGranuleShift, width: SlabArenaCommitGranuleBytes)

	// ⭐⭐ **A GRANULE'S CHUNKS MUST ALL LIE IN ONE BITMAP WORD, which is what makes the scavenger's
	// "is this granule entirely free?" ONE masked compare rather than a nested loop.** It holds with 56 bits
	// of headroom today (8 chunks in a 64-bit word) and is asserted because widening the granule is exactly
	// the change that would quietly turn that compare into a wrong answer about the granule's second half.
	//
	// ⚠ **`<=` IS THE WHOLE TEST ONLY BECAUSE BOTH ARE POWERS OF TWO, and that premise is established two
	// lines up rather than assumed here.** A granule's first chunk is `g · granuleChunks`, so its bit
	// position is `(g · granuleChunks) mod BitmapBitsPerWord`, which is a multiple of `granuleChunks` — and a
	// field of `granuleChunks` bits starting at a multiple of `granuleChunks` fits inside the word exactly
	// when `granuleChunks` DIVIDES the word width. `requireShiftMatchesWidth` above proves the granule is a
	// power of two and this proves it is no wider than the word, and for two powers of two those two
	// together are divisibility. A granule of, say, 24 chunks would pass `<=` and still straddle.
	if SlabArenaCommitGranuleChunks > BitmapBitsPerWord 'granuleSpansBitmapWords'
		panic("slab arena: a commit granule of {SlabArenaCommitGranuleChunks} chunks does not fit one {BitmapBitsPerWord}-bit bitmap word, so the scavenger's all-free test would miss the chunks past the first word")
	end 'granuleSpansBitmapWords'

	// ⭐ **AND THE GRANULE IS A WHOLE NUMBER OF THE WIDEST PAGE ANY LANE HAS.** Every range this file names to
	// the OS is a whole number of granules, so this is what makes each one page-aligned everywhere at once —
	// `mmap` REFUSES a length that is not, and `memory.grow` silently rounds one up.
	if SlabArenaCommitGranuleBytes mod LargestTargetPageBytes != 0 'granuleSplitsAPage'
		panic("slab arena: a commit granule of {SlabArenaCommitGranuleBytes} bytes is not a whole number of {LargestTargetPageBytes}-byte pages, so a committed range would split a page on the widest-page lane")
	end 'granuleSplitsAPage'

	// The non-reserving lane's grow unit IS one of the lanes' page sizes, so it can never be wider than the
	// widest of them. Asserted because the two constants hold the same number today and
	// `checkArenaReservationSlack` reads THIS one: a grow unit left stale by a lane change would make that
	// check answer for a page source no lane has, which is the shape its own header calls failing open.
	if UnreservedLaneGrowUnitBytes > LargestTargetPageBytes 'growUnitAboveWidestPage'
		panic("slab arena: the non-reserving lane's {UnreservedLaneGrowUnitBytes}-byte grow unit is wider than the {LargestTargetPageBytes}-byte widest page any lane has, so one of the two is stale")
	end 'growUnitAboveWidestPage'

	// `__slab_arena_new` clears the metadata chunks' bits by storing `-(1 shl ArenaMetadataChunks)` into
	// bitmap word 0, which is only the right answer while those bits all live in word 0. A fact about the
	// metadata reservation and the word width, and about neither arena's size.
	if ArenaMetadataChunks >= BitmapBitsPerWord 'metadataSpansWords'
		panic("slab arena: {ArenaMetadataChunks} metadata chunks do not fit in one {BitmapBitsPerWord}-bit bitmap word")
	end 'metadataSpansWords'

	// `__slab_arena_new` commits ONE granule and then writes the metadata into it, so the metadata chunks must
	// lie inside that first granule. Short, and the arena would initialise its own bitmap through a pointer
	// into uncommitted — on Windows and on a `PROT_NONE` POSIX reservation alike, INACCESSIBLE — memory.
	if SlabArenaCommitGranuleChunks < ArenaMetadataChunks 'metadataPastFirstGranule'
		panic("slab arena: {ArenaMetadataChunks} metadata chunk(s) do not fit in the first commit granule of {SlabArenaCommitGranuleChunks} chunk(s)")
	end 'metadataPastFirstGranule'

	// The four fields of an address must TILE it. Short, and a page's worth of addresses at the top of the
	// space would index nothing; long, and the L1 index would run past its table.
	let mappedBits = SlabPageShift + ArenaMapLevelBits + ArenaMapLevelBits + ArenaMapL1Bits
	if mappedBits != ArenaMapAddressBits 'addressBitsDisagree'
		panic("slab arena map: {SlabPageShift} + {ArenaMapLevelBits} + {ArenaMapLevelBits} + {ArenaMapL1Bits} is {mappedBits} bits, not the {ArenaMapAddressBits} the map claims to cover")
	end 'addressBitsDisagree'

	checkArenaGeometryForLane(SlabArenaGeometry.forReservation(true))
	checkArenaGeometryForLane(SlabArenaGeometry.forReservation(false))
end 'checkSlabArenaGeometry'

// ⭐⭐ **WHAT ONE LANE'S ARENA MUST SATISFY, ASKED OF BOTH — because a rule that stops applying to the lane
// nobody happens to be compiling is the "check that never runs" one signature up, wearing a target's
// clothes.** Every panic here names the lane, since the two geometries produce the same sentence with
// different numbers and a reader who cannot tell them apart has to re-derive which arena broke.
function checkArenaGeometryForLane(geometry SlabArenaGeometry)
	let lane = arenaLaneName(geometry.hasPageReservation)

	// The bitmap's width is the chunk count shifted down by a word's bit count, and a LOSSY shift would leave
	// the top chunks with no bit at all — free forever, and handed out by nobody. The scan would still work,
	// which is what makes it worth asserting.
	if geometry.bitmapWords * BitmapBitsPerWord != geometry.chunkCount 'bitmapShort'
		panic("slab arena ({lane}): {geometry.bitmapWords} bitmap words describe {geometry.bitmapWords * BitmapBitsPerWord} chunks, not the arena's {geometry.chunkCount}")
	end 'bitmapShort'

	// The granule is a whole divisor of the arena — without it, the last granule's commit would run past the
	// reservation and the OS would refuse it, and the derived granule count would describe fewer chunks than
	// the arena has. (That the granule is itself a whole number of chunks is asked of the granule alone, one
	// signature up.)
	if geometry.granuleCount * SlabArenaCommitGranuleChunks != geometry.chunkCount 'granuleOverrunsArena'
		panic("slab arena ({lane}): {geometry.granuleCount} commit granules describe {geometry.granuleCount * SlabArenaCommitGranuleChunks} chunks, not the arena's {geometry.chunkCount}")
	end 'granuleOverrunsArena'

	// The commit bitmap is a CEILING, so it may describe MORE granules than the arena has — never fewer, which
	// would leave the top granules with no commit bit and the claim path re-committing a live range on every
	// call that reached them.
	if geometry.commitBitmapWords * BitmapBitsPerWord < geometry.granuleCount 'commitBitmapShort'
		panic("slab arena ({lane}): {geometry.commitBitmapWords} commit-bitmap words describe {geometry.commitBitmapWords * BitmapBitsPerWord} granules, fewer than the arena's {geometry.granuleCount}")
	end 'commitBitmapShort'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE SMALLEST ARENA MUST STILL SATISFY THE LARGEST RUN THIS FILE ASKS OF ITSELF, AND SHRINKING AN
	// ARENA IS EXACTLY HOW THAT STOPS BEING TRUE.** The reverse map's level tables are allocated out of an
	// arena, and an L2/L3 level is the biggest single request the page layer makes. An arena too small for it
	// does not fail to compile: `__slab_arena_alloc_chunks` REFUSES the request at run time with
	// `slabChunkRunUnsatisfiable`, on one lane, in a build whose native twin is perfect. It subsumes the
	// weaker "an arena has any payload chunks at all", which is why that is not asked separately.
	let largestMapTableRun = arenaChunksFor(ArenaMapLevelEntries * ArenaWordBytes)
	if largestMapTableRun > geometry.chunkCount - ArenaMetadataChunks 'mapTableRunUnsatisfiable'
		panic("slab arena ({lane}): a {largestMapTableRun}-chunk reverse-map level does not fit the {geometry.chunkCount - ArenaMetadataChunks} chunks an arena has after its {ArenaMetadataChunks} metadata chunk(s), so `__slab_arena_map_ensure` would abort on the first lookup")
	end 'mapTableRunUnsatisfiable'

	// The list link and the two bitmaps are the whole of the metadata chunk — the commit bitmap is last, so
	// its end is what can overflow — and nothing may silently start overlapping the payload of chunk 0 if
	// either widens.
	let metadataBytes = (geometry.commitBitmapOffset as ParsedInt) + geometry.commitBitmapWords * ArenaWordBytes
	if metadataBytes > ArenaMetadataChunks * SlabPageBytes 'metadataOverflowsChunk'
		panic("slab arena ({lane}): {metadataBytes} bytes of metadata do not fit in {ArenaMetadataChunks} chunk(s) of {SlabPageBytes}")
	end 'metadataOverflowsChunk'

	checkArenaReservationSlack(geometry, lane: lane)
	checkArenaMapCoupling(geometry.chunkCount, hasPageReservation: geometry.hasPageReservation, lane: lane)
end 'checkArenaGeometryForLane'

// ⭐⭐ **THE RESERVATION MUST STILL HOLD THE ARENA AFTER THE ALIGN-UP HAS MOVED IT — asked as TWO rules,
// because the two lanes satisfy it for opposite reasons and one rule covering both would hold vacuously for
// whichever lane it was not written for.** Short by a byte and the arena's last granule is committed OUTSIDE
// the reservation, which on the POSIX lanes is a `MAP_FIXED` re-map over whatever the process had there — a
// clobber, not a refusal, and the one failure this geometry could produce that no abort would ever name.
function checkArenaReservationSlack(geometry SlabArenaGeometry, lane String)
	if not geometry.hasPageReservation 'noSlackToSpend'
		// The lane pays for its reservation, so it reserves EXACTLY the arena — which is only sound because
		// its page source hands out whole `UnreservedLaneGrowUnitBytes` units and a granule divides one,
		// making the align-up the identity. That divisibility is the premise, and it is arithmetic.
		if UnreservedLaneGrowUnitBytes mod SlabArenaCommitGranuleBytes != 0 'growUnitIsNotWholeGranules'
			panic("slab arena ({lane}): a page source handing out {UnreservedLaneGrowUnitBytes}-byte units does not answer a whole number of {SlabArenaCommitGranuleBytes}-byte commit granules, so `__slab_arena_new`'s align-up could move a base this lane reserves no room for")
		end 'growUnitIsNotWholeGranules'

		if geometry.reservationBytes != geometry.bytes 'unreservedLaneOverReserves'
			panic("slab arena ({lane}): reserving {geometry.reservationBytes} bytes for a {geometry.bytes}-byte arena charges this lane for slack its already-aligned base never uses")
		end 'unreservedLaneOverReserves'

		return
	end 'noSlackToSpend'

	// A lane with reservations pays only address space, so it buys the worst case outright: an align-up can
	// move the base by as much as one granule less a byte.
	if geometry.reservationBytes - geometry.bytes < SlabArenaCommitGranuleBytes - 1 'slackTooShort'
		panic("slab arena ({lane}): a {geometry.reservationBytes}-byte reservation leaves {geometry.reservationBytes - geometry.bytes} bytes of slack for a {geometry.bytes}-byte arena, fewer than the {SlabArenaCommitGranuleBytes - 1} an align-up to a commit granule can consume")
	end 'slackTooShort'
end 'checkArenaReservationSlack'

// ⭐⭐ **THE ARENA↔MAP-TABLE RATIO, WHICH IS THE ONE INVARIANT THE TWO LANES DO NOT SHARE — SO IT IS ASKED AS
// TWO RULES RATHER THAN AS ONE THAT HOLDS VACUOUSLY.** Where a reservation is free the arena IS one L3
// table's span (the identity `ReservedArenaChunkCount` is written as), and the map costs 0.1–0.2%. Where it
// is not, the arena is deliberately SMALLER than a table and what has to hold instead is that a whole number
// of them tile one — which is what makes `UnreservedArenaChunkCount`'s contiguity argument arithmetic rather
// than a hope. Collapsing the pair into the weaker rule would let the reserving lane silently shrink to an
// eighth of its table and lose the 0.1% without anything noticing.
//
// ⚠ **IT TAKES THE CHUNK COUNT RATHER THAN THE GEOMETRY, AND THAT IS THE BOOTSTRAP'S RULE AND NOT A STYLE
// CHOICE.** The `mod` needs a divisor whose type excludes 0. shv2 reads that off `ArenaChunkCount` through a
// FIELD access and is satisfied; the C# bootstrap — which is what compiles this file — is not, and answers
// E3057 for `ArenaMapLevelEntries mod geometry.chunkCount` while accepting the identical expression when the
// same ranged value arrives as a PARAMETER. Passing the field through a parameter is what makes the two
// compilers agree, so do not "simplify" this back to taking the geometry until they do.
function checkArenaMapCoupling(chunkCount ArenaChunkCount, hasPageReservation bool, lane String)
	if not hasPageReservation 'tilesATable'
		if ArenaMapLevelEntries mod chunkCount != 0 'arenaStraddlesTables'
			panic("slab arena ({lane}): an L3 table's {ArenaMapLevelEntries} pages are not a whole number of {chunkCount}-chunk arenas, so adjacent arenas would straddle tables and the map's cost is not the ~0.1% `UnreservedArenaChunkCount` claims")
		end 'arenaStraddlesTables'

		return
	end 'tilesATable'

	if chunkCount != ArenaMapLevelEntries 'arenaIsNotOneTable'
		panic("slab arena ({lane}): an arena of {chunkCount} chunks is not one L3 table's {ArenaMapLevelEntries} pages, so a table would describe more address space than the arena it was allocated for")
	end 'arenaIsNotOneTable'
end 'checkArenaMapCoupling'

// Which arena a panic above is talking about. The two geometries produce the same sentences with different
// numbers, and "512 KiB, not 64 MiB" is only readable once the reader is told which one was expected.
function arenaLaneName(hasPageReservation bool) returns String
	if hasPageReservation 'reserves'
		return "reserving lanes"
	end 'reserves'

	return "the non-reserving lane"
end 'arenaLaneName'

// A width and the shift that divides by it are ONE fact written twice, by necessity — this file has four
// such pairs, and the emitted code reaches each pair through whichever half its own expression happened to
// name. `checkSlabClassTable` asserts exactly this about `SlabPageShift`/`SlabPageBytes` and states the
// reason in full: a change to one half leaves the other silently describing a different geometry.
function requireShiftMatchesWidth(what String, shift ParsedInt, width ParsedInt)
	let produced = 1 shl shift
	if produced != width 'disagrees'
		panic("slab arena: {what}: 1 shl {shift} is {produced}, not {width}")
	end 'disagrees'
end 'requireShiftMatchesWidth'

// Chunks a byte count occupies, rounded up. The reverse map's three table sizes are the only caller, and
// they are compile-time constants — this runs in the COMPILER, so the runtime pays a `const` and no divide.
function arenaChunksFor(bytes ParsedInt) returns ParsedInt
	return (bytes + SlabPageBytes - 1) / SlabPageBytes
end 'arenaChunksFor'

// ⭐ **THE LARGEST RUN `__slab_arena_alloc_chunks` CAN EVER SATISFY ON A LANE.** Exported because the OBJECT
// layer is what makes the requests that have to fit — a span's chunk run and the allocator's own state
// region — and it had no way to ask. `checkSlabRuntimeGeometry` asks it of BOTH lanes for
// `checkArenaGeometryForLane`'s reason: a request too large is refused at RUN TIME with
// `slabChunkRunUnsatisfiable`, on one lane, in a build whose native twin is perfect.
export function slabArenaAllocatableChunks(hasPageReservation bool) returns ParsedInt
	return SlabArenaGeometry.forReservation(hasPageReservation).chunkCount - ArenaMetadataChunks
end 'slabArenaAllocatableChunks'

// `__slab_assert_os_alloc(ptr) -> ptr` — surface a refused OS page call as a clean abort, and hand the
// pointer on when it was not refused.
//
// ⭐ **WHY IT EXISTS AT ALL.** Every caller in this file stores INTO the pointer it just got back, so a
// failure that is not caught here arrives as `mov %reg, (<garbage>)`: an opaque access violation with no
// attribution, in the one condition a user is most likely to hit for a reason outside their own program. v1
// names the same guard and the same argument. It guards all three page calls this file makes — the
// reservation, the commit, and (through `SlabRuntime`) an OS-direct allocation — which is why its answer is
// its argument: a guard that hands the value on cannot be forgotten at a call site.
//
// ⛔⛔ **THE TEST IS `ptr <= 0`, AND `ptr == 0` WAS A WINDOWS-ONLY TEST WEARING A PORTABLE ONE'S CLOTHES.**
// `VirtualAlloc` answers NULL on failure, so 0 is right there. A RAW POSIX SYSCALL DOES NOT: `mmap` answers
// a small NEGATIVE ERRNO in the result register (−ENOMEM is −12), which the `== 0` test waves through as a
// valid base — and the caller then stores metadata to address −12. Testing the SIGN catches every one of
// them at once, and costs the same single compare, because no lane can answer a legitimate page base that is
// zero or negative: user-space addresses are positive on all three native lanes, and wasm's `memory.grow`
// base is past the module's own `.data`.
function buildSlabAssertOsAlloc(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SlabAssertOsAllocName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var oom = module.addBlock(func, label: b"oom")
	var ok = module.addBlock(func, label: b"ok")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let ptr = 0 as ValueId

	let lowestFailureAnswer = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let failed = emitCmp(module, block: entry, a: ptr, b: lowestFailureAnswer, pred: StdCmpPred.le, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(failed, thenBlockId: oom.id, elseBlockId: ok.id))

	emitRuntimeAbort(module, func: func, block: oom, abort: RuntimeAbort.slabOsAllocFailed, ids: ids)

	module.setTerminator(ok, op: StdOp.ret(ptr))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSlabAssertOsAlloc'

// `__slab_arena_new() -> arenaBase` — map an arena, initialise its metadata and bitmap, prepend it to the
// arena list, and answer its base.
//
// ⭐⭐ **v1 HAS THIS BODY TWICE, VERBATIM** — once as `__slab_arena_init` and once inside the `grow` branch
// of `__slab_arena_alloc_chunks`, including the `-2` qword-0 constant and the `1 upto 128` fill. Two copies
// of a bitmap initializer is two chances to leave an arena's chunk 0 marked FREE, which hands the metadata
// out as payload and corrupts the arena that owns it. It is written once here and called from both.
//
// ⭐ **THE BITMAP FILL IS ONE `memFill`, AND THE ALTERNATIVES ARE BOTH WORSE AT THIS SIZE.** v1 emits a
// RUNTIME loop over its 128 words. An earlier draft of this file unrolled the loop in the COMPILER instead,
// which was right at four words and is 128 emitted stores now that the arena is sized to the map's bottom
// level. `StdOp.memFill` (F1b) is neither: one op, lowering to the backend's own size ladder, and the count
// stays derived — which is what let the arena acquire a SECOND size without this line changing at all.
//
// ⚠ The fill writes ONES — `ArenaBitmapAllFreeByte` — because a set bit means FREE here, and a freshly
// committed mapping is ZERO. That is the one place the zeroing contract works against this file rather than
// for it, and it is why the fill is not merely skipped.
//
// The mapping is fresh, so every byte of it is zero (see `SlabRuntime`'s header, which states the contract
// this layer's chunks are the source of): the `next` link needs no explicit 0 when the list is empty, but it
// is written anyway because the list head is usually NOT 0 and the same store serves both cases.
//
// ⭐⭐ **IT RESERVES THE WHOLE ARENA AND COMMITS ONE GRANULE (F1c).** The reservation is the arena; the commit
// is what makes the metadata chunk writable. Everything past the first granule stays
// reserved-and-inaccessible until `__slab_arena_alloc_chunks` hands out a chunk that reaches it, which is the
// whole reason a 64 MiB arena is affordable on a lane that has reservations — and, where there is no such
// thing, exactly the reason the arena is 512 KiB instead. See the header.
function buildSlabArenaNew(module StdModule, geometry SlabArenaGeometry)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SlabArenaNewName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 0, paramTypes: StdTypeArray.create(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(0)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")

	let requested = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: geometry.reservationBytes, ids: ids)
	let raw = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.osReservePages(raw, sizeId: requested))
	let reserved = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.call(reserved, callee: SlabAssertOsAllocName, args: argsOf(raw)))

	// ⛔⛔ **THE ARENA STARTS AT THE NEXT COMMIT GRANULE, NOT AT THE ADDRESS THE OS ANSWERED — AND TAKING
	// THE OS's ANSWER WAS AN UNSTATED ALIGNMENT ASSUMPTION THAT THE LANES WITH A 4 KiB PAGE DO NOT SATISFY.**
	// `osReservePages` promises the LANE'S page granularity and nothing more: 64 KiB on Windows (the
	// allocation granularity) and on wasm (the grow unit), 16 KiB on arm64-macOS — but **4 KiB on BOTH LINUX
	// LANES, HALF a chunk** (`ElfWriter.ElfPageSize` is the 0x1000 they share). The reverse map indexes by
	// `addr shr SlabPageShift`, so an arena whose base is 4096 mod 8192 puts the chunk grid out of phase with
	// the map's page grid, and the map then describes a heap that is not the one the allocator hands out:
	//
	//   • `__slab_arena_map_set(spanBase, numPages, span)` registers exactly `numPages` page indices while
	//     the span's BYTE range touches `numPages + 1` of them — so the tail of every span is unregistered,
	//     `__slab_free` MISSES there, reads the pointer as OS-direct and aborts
	//     (`slabOsDirectPointerNotOurs`);
	//   • and each registered index covers the first bytes of its page, which belong to the PRECEDING chunk
	//     — so a slot at the end of one span resolves to its NEIGHBOUR's span record and is pushed onto the
	//     wrong free list, which is a silent wrong answer and then a fault.
	//
	// MEASURED (W215) on x64-linux: `spec-test --target=x64-linux --filter=slab` failed 4 of 16 on one run
	// and a DIFFERENT 6 of 16 on the next, because the set moved with ASLR; a probe aborting on a misaligned
	// base showed the failing runs were exactly the ones where `mmap` answered 4096 mod 8192, and
	// `setarch -R` — which pins the base — failed 15 of 15. **The SCOPE is wider than the measurement:**
	// arm64-linux is the same 4 KiB page reached through the same raw `mmap`, and is cured by the same
	// statement; it is not separately measured because that lane's runs are remote.
	//
	// ⚠ **arm64-macOS WAS NOT AFFECTED, AND SAYING SO IS THE POINT OF NAMING THE PAGE SIZES ABOVE.** 16 KiB
	// is a whole multiple of a chunk, so that lane's `mmap` answer already put the chunk grid in phase and
	// every granule range it named was already page-aligned. What it did NOT satisfy is the stronger GRANULE
	// alignment established below — and nothing before required that, which is why this is a fix for the
	// 4 KiB lanes and a no-op everywhere else.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **THE GRANULE IS THE ALIGNMENT, NOT THE CHUNK, AND THAT IS ONE FACT COVERING BOTH OBLIGATIONS.**
	// Every range this file names to the OS is a whole granule at a granule offset from this base, so a
	// granule-aligned base is what keeps each one page-aligned on EVERY lane no matter which base the OS
	// answers — including arm64-macOS's 16 KiB page, which a merely CHUNK-aligned base would split (`mmap`
	// refuses that, and `checkSlabArenaGeometry` already asserts the granule is a whole number of the widest
	// page). A granule is also a whole number of chunks, so the map's grid follows from the same statement
	// instead of needing a second one.
	let base = emitAlignUp(module, block: entry, value: reserved, width: SlabArenaCommitGranuleBytes, ids: ids)

	// ⛔⛔ **`reserved` DIES HERE, AND THE DAY ANYTHING RELEASES AN ARENA IT IS THE VALUE THAT MUST COME
	// BACK.** Nothing in this file frees an arena — `__slab_arena_list` is prepended to and never unlinked,
	// and the only `osFreePages` the slab emits is `__slab_os_direct_free`'s, over an `osAllocPages` region
	// that never went through here — so today there is no address to keep and no place to keep it. **What
	// makes that worth writing down is that `base` is the value in scope and the wrong one to release:**
	// Windows' `VirtualFree(MEM_RELEASE)` REQUIRES the address `VirtualAlloc` answered and REFUSES any other,
	// and a POSIX `munmap(base, bytes)` would unmap the arena while leaking the align-up's head slack and the
	// reservation's tail. A teardown therefore has to carry `reserved` and `geometry.reservationBytes`, which
	// means storing the raw base in the metadata chunk — it cannot be recovered from `base`, since the
	// align-up is not injective. ⇒ **FOLLOW-UP, NAMED: an arena teardown starts by giving the metadata chunk
	// a `reservedBase` word.**
	//
	// ⭐ Everything the arena DOES name to the OS after this point stays inside the reservation without it:
	// the scavenger's decommit and `emitCommitGranulesFor`'s commit are both `[base + g·granule, +granule)`
	// for `g < granuleCount`, so their union is exactly `[base, base + bytes)`, and
	// `checkArenaReservationSlack` is what makes that a subrange of `[reserved, reserved + reservationBytes)`.

	// ⛔⛔ **AND THE ALIGNMENT IS CHECKED RATHER THAN ASSUMED, BECAUSE AN UNCHECKED COUPLING IS EXACTLY
	// WHAT THE DEFECT ABOVE WAS.** It was wrong 50% of the time, for two weeks, on a lane whose suite read
	// green on the other half of the coin flips — so the cure ends with the invariant stated to the machine
	// and not only to the reader. Three ops on a path that runs once per arena.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS NOT TAUTOLOGICAL EVEN THOUGH THE ROUND-UP IS UNCONDITIONAL.** Two things can still put a
	// misaligned base here, and neither is hypothetical in kind: a granule that is not a power of two, or a
	// backend materialising the `-granule` mask at the wrong WIDTH — a defect class this project has shipped
	// before (`project_x8_no_range_is_not_no_constraint`). And on the lane that reserves NO slack
	// (`arenaAlignSlackBytes` gives wasm zero, because `memory.grow` answers a whole-page base) a round-up
	// that actually moved the base would move it PAST the reservation's end; `checkArenaReservationSlack`
	// asserts that premise at compile time from the grow unit, and this is the same statement asked of the
	// address a future lane really returns.
	var misaligned = module.addBlock(func, label: b"misaligned")
	var aligned = module.addBlock(func, label: b"aligned")

	let granuleMask = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: SlabArenaCommitGranuleBytes - 1, ids: ids)
	let withinGranule = emitBin(module, block: entry, a: base, b: granuleMask, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitAnd, ids: ids)
	let onBoundary = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let offGrid = emitCmp(module, block: entry, a: withinGranule, b: onBoundary, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(offGrid, thenBlockId: misaligned.id, elseBlockId: aligned.id))

	emitRuntimeAbort(module, func: func, block: misaligned, abort: RuntimeAbort.slabArenaBaseNotGranuleAligned, ids: ids)

	// The first granule, before ANY store below: the metadata chunk is inside it (asserted by
	// `checkSlabArenaGeometry`), and until it is committed the arena's own base is not writable.
	let firstGranule = emitConst(module, block: aligned, value: SlabArenaCommitGranuleBytes, ids: ids)
	emitGuardedCommit(module, block: aligned, base: base, byteCount: firstGranule, ids: ids)

	// Prepend: `new.next = head; head = new`. The head address is read once and used twice — ordinary SSA,
	// and `globalAddr` is pure, so the allocator may rematerialize it rather than keep it live.
	let headAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(aligned, op: StdOp.globalAddr(headAddr, label: SlabArenaListLabel))
	let oldHead = emitLoadField(module, block: aligned, record: headAddr, offset: 0, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: aligned, record: base, offset: ArenaNextOffset, value: oldHead)

	let bitmapOffset = emitConst(module, block: aligned, value: ArenaBitmapOffset as ParsedInt, ids: ids)
	let bitmapAddr = emitBin(module, block: aligned, a: base, b: bitmapOffset, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let bitmapBytes = emitConst(module, block: aligned, value: geometry.bitmapWords * ArenaWordBytes, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(aligned, op: StdOp.memFill(bitmapAddr, byteCountId: bitmapBytes, value: ArenaBitmapAllFreeByte))

	// `-(1 shl k)` is the all-ones mask with its low `k` bits cleared — the metadata chunks marked USED, every
	// other chunk in word 0 free. It lands AFTER the fill, which wrote that word all-free a moment ago.
	let firstWord = emitConst(module, block: aligned, value: 0 - (1 shl ArenaMetadataChunks), ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: aligned, record: base, offset: ArenaBitmapOffset, value: firstWord)

	// ⭐ **GRANULE 0 IS COMMITTED AND EVERY OTHER GRANULE IS NOT, WHICH IS WHAT THE FRESH MAPPING ALREADY
	// SAYS.** The commit bitmap sits past the chunk bitmap the fill above stopped at, so it is still the
	// reservation's own zeroes — and a zero bit MEANS "not committed". Only the one bit the commit two dozen
	// lines up actually made true has to be written, exactly as the arena-list head's zero is its own
	// initialisation.
	let firstGranuleCommitted = emitConst(module, block: aligned, value: 1, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: aligned, record: base, offset: geometry.commitBitmapOffset, value: firstGranuleCommitted)

	emitStoreField(module, block: aligned, record: headAddr, offset: 0, value: base)
	module.setTerminator(aligned, op: StdOp.ret(base))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSlabArenaNew'

// `__slab_arena_alloc_chunks(numChunks) -> base` — first-fit over the arena list's bitmaps: find a run of
// `numChunks` consecutive free chunks, mark them used, and answer the address of the first. When no arena
// has room, map a fresh one and scan that.
//
// ⭐ **THE UNSATISFIABLE REQUEST IS REFUSED, WHICH v1 DOES NOT DO.** Its loop grows a fresh arena and
// rescans forever for a run no arena can ever hold, mapping memory until the OS refuses — an out-of-memory
// abort reported for a request that was simply too large. A count outside `[1, chunks - metadata]` is a
// COMPILER bug (a caller computed a span geometry wrong), and 0 is the quietest of them: the run test
// `runLen >= 0` succeeds before any bit is cleared, so the answer is a chunk that is still marked FREE and
// will be handed out a second time.
//
// ⭐ **THE ALL-USED WORD IS SKIPPED WHOLE.** A bitmap word of 0 has no free bit, so no run can start inside
// it and any run in progress ends at it — 64 bit tests collapse to one compare. It is what makes scanning a
// FULL arena, which is the case the list walk repeats for every arena it passes, cost `bitmapWords` loads
// instead of `chunkCount`. The test is asked only at a word boundary, so it costs one compare per 64 chunks
// on the path where it does not fire.
//
// ⚠ **THE WALK IS STILL O(arenas × chunks) PER CALL AND THAT IS A KNOWN SHAPE, NOT A HIDDEN ONE.** Go
// replaces it with a radix summary tree (`pageAlloc`) precisely because a linear list of bitmaps is the
// wrong data structure once arenas are many. It is left linear here because a chunk run is allocated ONCE
// PER SPAN and a span serves many objects, so the constant is amortised over the layer above — and because
// the summary tree is a data-structure rung, not a page-layer one.
//
// ⚠ **WHICH IS WHY THE ARENA'S SIZE IS A TRADE AND NOT A WIN, IN EITHER DIRECTION — MEASURE THE PRODUCT, NOT
// THE ROW.** F1c multiplied the per-arena reject cost by 32 (4 word loads → 128) and divided the arena count
// a heap of a given size needs by the same 32; the non-reserving lane's 512 KiB arena divides the reject cost
// by 128 (128 → 1) and multiplies the arena count by 128. **In both directions the PRODUCT — the work to walk
// a full heap — is unchanged**, which is what makes the geometry a memory decision rather than a speed one.
// The summary tree is what changes the product.
//
// ⭐ **AND THE CLAIM COMMITS WHAT IT REACHES.** The bits are marked used, then `emitCommitGranulesFor` backs
// every granule the run touches that is not backed already. It is one load, one shift and one test per
// granule on the path where nothing needs committing — and a run is one to three granules, a span being at
// most ten chunks — which is every call after the first that reaches a granule.
function buildSlabArenaAllocChunks(module StdModule, geometry SlabArenaGeometry)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SlabArenaAllocChunksName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var checkHigh = module.addBlock(func, label: b"checkhigh")
	var badRequest = module.addBlock(func, label: b"badreq")
	var start = module.addBlock(func, label: b"start")
	var arenaLoop = module.addBlock(func, label: b"arenaloop")
	var grow = module.addBlock(func, label: b"grow")
	var nextArena = module.addBlock(func, label: b"nextarena")
	var scanStart = module.addBlock(func, label: b"scanstart")
	var scanHeader = module.addBlock(func, label: b"scanh")
	var loadWord = module.addBlock(func, label: b"loadword")
	var maybeSkip = module.addBlock(func, label: b"maybeskip")
	var skipWord = module.addBlock(func, label: b"skipword")
	var testBit = module.addBlock(func, label: b"testbit")
	var breakRun = module.addBlock(func, label: b"breakrun")
	var extendRun = module.addBlock(func, label: b"extendrun")
	var newRun = module.addBlock(func, label: b"newrun")
	var growRun = module.addBlock(func, label: b"growrun")
	var checkRun = module.addBlock(func, label: b"checkrun")
	var resumeScan = module.addBlock(func, label: b"resumescan")
	var claim = module.addBlock(func, label: b"claim")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let numChunks = 0 as ValueId

	let minRun = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let tooSmall = emitCmp(module, block: entry, a: numChunks, b: minRun, pred: StdCmpPred.lt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(tooSmall, thenBlockId: badRequest.id, elseBlockId: checkHigh.id))

	let maxRun = emitConst(module, block: checkHigh, value: geometry.chunkCount - ArenaMetadataChunks, ids: ids)
	let tooBig = emitCmp(module, block: checkHigh, a: numChunks, b: maxRun, pred: StdCmpPred.gt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(checkHigh, op: StdOp.condBranch(tooBig, thenBlockId: badRequest.id, elseBlockId: start.id))

	emitRuntimeAbort(module, func: func, block: badRequest, abort: RuntimeAbort.slabChunkRunUnsatisfiable, ids: ids)

	let headAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(start, op: StdOp.globalAddr(headAddr, label: SlabArenaListLabel))
	let head = emitLoadField(module, block: start, record: headAddr, offset: 0, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(start, op: StdOp.branch(arenaLoop.id))
	start.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(arenaLoop.id, argIds: argsOf(head)))

	// The arena under the scan. Every block below reaches it by DOMINANCE, so only the three back edges
	// (the list walk, the grow retry, the initial entry) carry it.
	let arena = ids.mint()
	arenaLoop.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(arena, argType: StdType.i64))

	let noArena = emitConst(module, block: arenaLoop, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let exhausted = emitCmp(module, block: arenaLoop, a: arena, b: noArena, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(arenaLoop, op: StdOp.condBranch(exhausted, thenBlockId: grow.id, elseBlockId: scanStart.id))

	// Grow: map a fresh arena and rescan against IT. `__slab_arena_new` prepends, so the retry starts at the
	// arena that was just mapped rather than re-walking the ones that had no room.
	let fresh = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(grow, op: StdOp.call(fresh, callee: SlabArenaNewName, args: ValueIdArray.create()))
	module.setTerminator(grow, op: StdOp.branch(arenaLoop.id))
	grow.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(arenaLoop.id, argIds: argsOf(fresh)))

	let following = emitLoadField(module, block: nextArena, record: arena, offset: ArenaNextOffset, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(nextArena, op: StdOp.branch(arenaLoop.id))
	nextArena.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(arenaLoop.id, argIds: argsOf(following)))

	let firstChunk = emitConst(module, block: scanStart, value: ArenaMetadataChunks, ids: ids)
	let noRun = emitConst(module, block: scanStart, value: 0, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(scanStart, op: StdOp.branch(scanHeader.id))
	scanStart.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(scanHeader.id, argIds: threeArgs(firstChunk, second: noRun, third: noRun)))

	// The scan's three carried values: the chunk under test, the length of the free run ending just before
	// it, and where that run started. `runStart` is meaningful only while `runLen > 0`, which is why the
	// break and skip edges leave it alone rather than inventing a value for it.
	let chunk = ids.mint()
	let runLen = ids.mint()
	let runStart = ids.mint()
	scanHeader.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(chunk, argType: StdType.i64))
	scanHeader.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(runLen, argType: StdType.i64))
	scanHeader.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(runStart, argType: StdType.i64))

	let chunkLimit = emitConst(module, block: scanHeader, value: geometry.chunkCount, ids: ids)
	let scanned = emitCmp(module, block: scanHeader, a: chunk, b: chunkLimit, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(scanHeader, op: StdOp.condBranch(scanned, thenBlockId: nextArena.id, elseBlockId: loadWord.id))

	let wordAddr = emitBitmapWordAddr(module, block: loadWord, arenaBase: arena, bitIndex: chunk, ids: ids)
	let word = emitLoadField(module, block: loadWord, record: wordAddr, offset: ArenaBitmapOffset, ids: ids)
	let bitPos = emitBitPosition(module, block: loadWord, bitIndex: chunk, ids: ids)
	let atWordStart = emitCmp(module, block: loadWord, a: bitPos, b: noRun, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(loadWord, op: StdOp.condBranch(atWordStart, thenBlockId: maybeSkip.id, elseBlockId: testBit.id))

	let wordFull = emitCmp(module, block: maybeSkip, a: word, b: noRun, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(maybeSkip, op: StdOp.condBranch(wordFull, thenBlockId: skipWord.id, elseBlockId: testBit.id))

	let wordWidth = emitConst(module, block: skipWord, value: BitmapBitsPerWord, ids: ids)
	let pastWord = emitBin(module, block: skipWord, a: chunk, b: wordWidth, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(skipWord, op: StdOp.branch(scanHeader.id))
	skipWord.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(scanHeader.id, argIds: threeArgs(pastWord, second: noRun, third: runStart)))

	let bit = emitSelectBit(module, block: testBit, word: word, bitPos: bitPos, ids: ids)
	let oneBit = emitConst(module, block: testBit, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let isFree = emitCmp(module, block: testBit, a: bit, b: noRun, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(testBit, op: StdOp.condBranch(isFree, thenBlockId: extendRun.id, elseBlockId: breakRun.id))

	let afterUsed = emitBin(module, block: breakRun, a: chunk, b: oneBit, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(breakRun, op: StdOp.branch(scanHeader.id))
	breakRun.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(scanHeader.id, argIds: threeArgs(afterUsed, second: noRun, third: runStart)))

	let runIsNew = emitCmp(module, block: extendRun, a: runLen, b: noRun, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(extendRun, op: StdOp.condBranch(runIsNew, thenBlockId: newRun.id, elseBlockId: growRun.id))

	let afterFirst = emitBin(module, block: newRun, a: chunk, b: oneBit, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(newRun, op: StdOp.branch(checkRun.id))
	newRun.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(checkRun.id, argIds: threeArgs(afterFirst, second: oneBit, third: chunk)))

	let longerRun = emitBin(module, block: growRun, a: runLen, b: oneBit, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let afterNext = emitBin(module, block: growRun, a: chunk, b: oneBit, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(growRun, op: StdOp.branch(checkRun.id))
	growRun.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(checkRun.id, argIds: threeArgs(afterNext, second: longerRun, third: runStart)))

	let nextChunk = ids.mint()
	let currentLen = ids.mint()
	let currentStart = ids.mint()
	checkRun.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(nextChunk, argType: StdType.i64))
	checkRun.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(currentLen, argType: StdType.i64))
	checkRun.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(currentStart, argType: StdType.i64))

	let longEnough = emitCmp(module, block: checkRun, a: currentLen, b: numChunks, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(checkRun, op: StdOp.condBranch(longEnough, thenBlockId: claim.id, elseBlockId: resumeScan.id))

	// The scan continues through an unconditional branch rather than off `checkRun`'s conditional, so that
	// every edge carrying block arguments leaves a single-successor block. Same shape at every other
	// arg-carrying edge in this function.
	module.setTerminator(resumeScan, op: StdOp.branch(scanHeader.id))
	resumeScan.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(scanHeader.id, argIds: threeArgs(nextChunk, second: currentLen, third: currentStart)))

	// The run's END is computed ONCE here and read by both halves of the claim — the bit walk's bound and the
	// commit's watermark test are the same address, and computing it twice is how the two come to disagree
	// about where a run stops.
	let pastRun = emitBin(module, block: claim, a: currentStart, b: numChunks, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	var claimed = emitBitRunUpdate(module, func: func, cur: claim, arenaBase: arena, firstChunk: currentStart, pastChunk: pastRun, mode: SlabBitRun.claim, ids: ids)

	// The run is the caller's now, but the arena only RESERVED its address space, and the scavenger may since
	// have dropped the backing of any granule the run lands in — so every granule it touches has to be backed
	// before the caller writes into it.
	var backed = emitCommitGranulesFor(module, func: func, cur: claimed, arenaBase: arena, firstChunk: currentStart, pastChunk: pastRun, geometry: geometry, ids: ids)

	let pageShift = emitConst(module, block: backed, value: SlabPageShift, ids: ids)
	let runOffset = emitBin(module, block: backed, a: currentStart, b: pageShift, opcode: StdBinOpcode.shl, ids: ids)
	let runBase = emitBin(module, block: backed, a: arena, b: runOffset, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(backed, op: StdOp.ret(runBase))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSlabArenaAllocChunks'

// `__slab_arena_free_chunks(arenaBase, chunkIndex, numChunks)` — ZERO a chunk run and mark it free again.
//
// ⭐ **THE RANGE IS CHECKED, WHICH v1 DOES NOT DO.** An index below the metadata chunks would free the
// arena's own bitmap into the pool; one past the end writes past the bitmap into chunk 0's payload. Both
// are silent — the corruption surfaces later, somewhere else, as a chunk handed out twice.
//
// ⭐⭐⭐ **INV-4 IS ESTABLISHED HERE AND NOWHERE ELSE: EVERY CHUNK `__slab_arena_alloc_chunks` HANDS OUT
// READS ZERO.** Before S6 that was true for a reason nobody had to write down — a chunk had only ever come
// from a fresh `osReservePages`/`osCommitPages`, which every lane zeroes. The scavenger recycles chunks, and
// a recycled chunk holds the previous span's slots: poisoned payloads, and in each box header a REFCOUNT.
// `__slab_refill` cuts a span whose whole bump region is presumed zero (INV-3) and `__mm_alloc` does not
// write the refcount because 0 is the born state — so an unzeroed recycled chunk is a heap that frees boxes
// at the wrong time, with no diagnostic.
//
// ⛔⛔ **THE ZERO IS WRITTEN ON THE RELEASE PATH, NOT RECOVERED FROM THE OS ON THE CLAIM PATH, AND THAT IS
// THE WHOLE DIFFERENCE BETWEEN THIS AND THE TRAP `StdOp.osDecommitPages` WARNS ABOUT.** What a recommitted
// page contains is FOUR different answers (Windows zeroes, Linux refaults zero, Darwin's `MADV_FREE` may
// return the SAME DIRTY PAGE, wasm keeps the bytes verbatim), and the failure shape that op names is a
// future `pagesAreZeroAfterReback` flag set false for macOS and forgotten for wasm. **There is no such flag
// to write here**: the zero is ours, written into committed memory BEFORE any decommit can touch it, so
// every one of those four answers gives the chunk back holding zeroes — Darwin's dirty page is dirty with
// OUR zeroes, and wasm's verbatim bytes are the same zeroes. One unconditional `memFill`, no lane test, and
// nothing for anyone to make conditional later.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS SAFE TO WRITE, WHICH IS NOT OBVIOUS.** The run is being RELEASED, so it was CLAIMED, so
// `emitCommitGranulesFor` backed every granule it touches and the scavenger cannot have dropped one (a
// granule holding a claimed chunk is not fully free). The range is therefore committed at this instant on
// every lane.
function buildSlabArenaFreeChunks(module StdModule, geometry SlabArenaGeometry)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SlabArenaFreeChunksName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 3, paramTypes: threeI64Params(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(3)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var checkCount = module.addBlock(func, label: b"checkcount")
	var checkHigh = module.addBlock(func, label: b"checkhigh")
	var badRange = module.addBlock(func, label: b"badrange")
	var release = module.addBlock(func, label: b"release")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 3)
	let arenaBase = 0 as ValueId
	let chunkIndex = 1 as ValueId
	let numChunks = 2 as ValueId

	let firstAllocatable = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: ArenaMetadataChunks, ids: ids)
	let belowArena = emitCmp(module, block: entry, a: chunkIndex, b: firstAllocatable, pred: StdCmpPred.lt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(belowArena, thenBlockId: badRange.id, elseBlockId: checkCount.id))

	let minRun = emitConst(module, block: checkCount, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let emptyRun = emitCmp(module, block: checkCount, a: numChunks, b: minRun, pred: StdCmpPred.lt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(checkCount, op: StdOp.condBranch(emptyRun, thenBlockId: badRange.id, elseBlockId: checkHigh.id))

	let pastRun = emitBin(module, block: checkHigh, a: chunkIndex, b: numChunks, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let chunkLimit = emitConst(module, block: checkHigh, value: geometry.chunkCount, ids: ids)
	let aboveArena = emitCmp(module, block: checkHigh, a: pastRun, b: chunkLimit, pred: StdCmpPred.gt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(checkHigh, op: StdOp.condBranch(aboveArena, thenBlockId: badRange.id, elseBlockId: release.id))

	emitRuntimeAbort(module, func: func, block: badRange, abort: RuntimeAbort.slabChunkRangeOutOfArena, ids: ids)

	// INV-4: the run reads zero from here on, whatever happens to its backing afterwards. It lands BEFORE the
	// bits are cleared, so no concurrent claim can ever observe a free-but-dirty chunk.
	let pageShift = emitConst(module, block: release, value: SlabPageShift, ids: ids)
	let runOffset = emitBin(module, block: release, a: chunkIndex, b: pageShift, opcode: StdBinOpcode.shl, ids: ids)
	let runBase = emitBin(module, block: release, a: arenaBase, b: runOffset, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let runBytes = emitBin(module, block: release, a: numChunks, b: pageShift, opcode: StdBinOpcode.shl, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(release, op: StdOp.memFill(runBase, byteCountId: runBytes, value: 0))

	// `pastRun` is the value `checkHigh` already computed for the range test — the same end, read again rather
	// than derived a second time.
	var released = emitBitRunUpdate(module, func: func, cur: release, arenaBase: arenaBase, firstChunk: chunkIndex, pastChunk: pastRun, mode: SlabBitRun.release, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(released, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSlabArenaFreeChunks'

// `__slab_arena_of(addr) -> arenaBase` — which arena's reservation contains `addr`.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THIS IS THE ANSWER TO THE QUESTION THIS FILE'S HEADER LEFT OPEN AS "THE SCAVENGER'S DEBT", AND IT
// STORES NOTHING.** `__slab_arena_free_chunks` needs an arena base its caller does not hold: a span records
// where its chunk run STARTS, not which arena it was cut from. The three candidates the header named were a
// real out-value on the alloc entry point, an arena level in the reverse map, and an aligned reservation the
// address could be masked to. This is a fourth, and it wins on the fact that decides between them — **it is
// asked at DESTRUCTION, not at BIRTH.** A span is destroyed at most once and created at least once, so a
// lookup here is strictly rarer than a field written there, and the field would have cost the mspan record
// its LAST free word (`MspanScavStateOffset` takes it) and pushed the header to 128 bytes.
//
// ⛔ **v1 STORES IT, AND THE MECHANISM IS WHY THAT IS NOT PORTED**: `__slab_arena_alloc_chunks` there writes
// the arena it served from into a `.data` word (`__slab_arena_last_base`) that its caller reads on the very
// next line — a hidden out-parameter, which makes a function's answer depend on a global the same call
// wrote, and which this file's no-lock note says is exactly the shape that stops working the day two OS
// threads reach the allocator.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS A LINEAR WALK AND THAT COSTS NOTHING IT DID NOT ALREADY COST.** The walk is over the arena list,
// which is the same list `__slab_arena_alloc_chunks` walks — and that one walks the arenas AND their
// bitmaps. Every address handed here came out of a chunk run whose allocation had just paid strictly more.
//
// ⚠ It ABORTS rather than answering a sentinel for an address in no arena: every caller is about to use the
// answer as a base to compute a chunk index from, and a 0 there would release chunk `addr >> 13` of the
// arena at address 0.
function buildSlabArenaOf(module StdModule, geometry SlabArenaGeometry)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SlabArenaOfName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var walk = module.addBlock(func, label: b"walk")
	var missing = module.addBlock(func, label: b"missing")
	var testLow = module.addBlock(func, label: b"testlow")
	var testHigh = module.addBlock(func, label: b"testhigh")
	var found = module.addBlock(func, label: b"found")
	var advance = module.addBlock(func, label: b"nextarena")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let addr = 0 as ValueId

	let headAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.globalAddr(headAddr, label: SlabArenaListLabel))
	let head = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: headAddr, offset: 0, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.branch(walk.id))
	entry.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(walk.id, argIds: argsOf(head)))

	let arena = ids.mint()
	walk.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(arena, argType: StdType.i64))

	let noArena = emitConst(module, block: walk, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let exhausted = emitCmp(module, block: walk, a: arena, b: noArena, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(walk, op: StdOp.condBranch(exhausted, thenBlockId: missing.id, elseBlockId: testLow.id))

	emitRuntimeAbort(module, func: func, block: missing, abort: RuntimeAbort.slabAddressOutsideEveryArena, ids: ids)

	let below = emitCmp(module, block: testLow, a: addr, b: arena, pred: StdCmpPred.lt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(testLow, op: StdOp.condBranch(below, thenBlockId: advance.id, elseBlockId: testHigh.id))

	let arenaBytes = emitConst(module, block: testHigh, value: geometry.bytes, ids: ids)
	let limit = emitBin(module, block: testHigh, a: arena, b: arenaBytes, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let above = emitCmp(module, block: testHigh, a: addr, b: limit, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(testHigh, op: StdOp.condBranch(above, thenBlockId: advance.id, elseBlockId: found.id))

	module.setTerminator(found, op: StdOp.ret(arena))

	let following = emitLoadField(module, block: advance, record: arena, offset: ArenaNextOffset, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(advance, op: StdOp.branch(walk.id))
	advance.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(walk.id, argIds: argsOf(following)))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSlabArenaOf'

// `__slab_arena_scavenge() -> bytesDecommitted` — drop the physical backing of every commit granule, in
// every arena, whose chunks are ALL free, and answer how many bytes that was.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE UNIT IS THE GRANULE AND NOT THE SPAN, AND THAT IS A CORRECTNESS ARGUMENT RATHER THAN A
// BATCHING ONE — IT IS ALSO WHERE THIS DIVERGES FROM v1 MOST SHARPLY.** v1's scavenger decommits a SPAN's
// own footprint, which is chunk-granular: `ceil(slotSize · totalSlots / 8192) · 8192`, at the span's base.
// **On a 16 KiB-page lane that range is neither aligned nor a whole number of pages**, and `madvise` answers
// one of two things to it — `EINVAL` for a base that is not page-aligned (a silent no-op, since nothing
// checks), or, for a base that happens to be aligned, a LENGTH ROUNDED UP that throws away the first 8 KiB
// of the NEXT span. arm64-macOS's page is 16 KiB, exactly twice a chunk. ⇒ a per-span decommit is a no-op
// or a corruption there, and green on x64 either way.
//
// The commit granule is 64 KiB precisely because it is a whole multiple of every page size these lanes have
// (`SlabArenaCommitGranuleBytes`), so a granule-aligned range of whole granules is legal on all of them from
// one target-neutral body. **Go answers the same question the same way** — its scavenger works on the PAGE
// ALLOCATOR's free runs and rounds them to `physPageSize`, with a `minPages = physPageSize / pageSize`
// floor (`vendor/go/src/runtime/mgcscavenge.go:749`) — rather than on spans.
//
// ⭐ **A GRANULE IS RELEASED ONLY WHEN EVERY CHUNK IN IT IS FREE**, which is what keeps the arena's own
// metadata, the allocator's state region and the reverse-map level tables backed: each of those is a CLAIMED
// chunk, and one claimed chunk in a granule is enough. Granule 0 can therefore never be released at all —
// it holds the metadata chunk — which is checked by nothing because it needs no check.
//
// ⚠ **ON wasm32-wasi `osDecommitPages` EMITS NOTHING, and this body still runs in full.** Linear memory only
// grows, so there is no backing to drop; the granule's commit bit still clears and is still re-set by the
// next claim that reaches it, and the chunks are still reusable by any class. That is a documented no-op
// with an ISA reason, and the byte count it answers is the bytes it handed back — on that lane, the bytes
// nothing was listening to.
//
// ⚠ **THE WALK IS O(arenas × granules) PER CALL, WHICH IS LINEAR IN THE HEAP AND IS THE SHAPE TO KEEP.** One
// granule is 64 KiB, so a 1 GiB heap is 16,384 iterations of four instructions. Go replaces the equivalent
// with a radix summary tree because its scavenger runs CONTINUOUSLY on a background goroutine; this one runs
// when a program asks, and a summary tree is a data-structure rung rather than a scavenger one — the same
// judgement `__slab_arena_alloc_chunks` records about its own linear scan.
function buildSlabArenaScavenge(module StdModule, geometry SlabArenaGeometry)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SlabArenaScavengeName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 0, paramTypes: StdTypeArray.create(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(0)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var arenaLoop = module.addBlock(func, label: b"arenaloop")
	var finish = module.addBlock(func, label: b"finish")
	var granuleStart = module.addBlock(func, label: b"granulestart")
	var granuleLoop = module.addBlock(func, label: b"granuleloop")
	var nextArena = module.addBlock(func, label: b"nextarena")
	var testCommit = module.addBlock(func, label: b"testcommit")
	var skipUncommitted = module.addBlock(func, label: b"skipuncommitted")
	var testFree = module.addBlock(func, label: b"testfree")
	var skipInUse = module.addBlock(func, label: b"skipinuse")
	var release = module.addBlock(func, label: b"release")

	let headAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.globalAddr(headAddr, label: SlabArenaListLabel))
	let head = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: headAddr, offset: 0, ids: ids)
	let nothing = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 0, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.branch(arenaLoop.id))
	entry.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(arenaLoop.id, argIds: twoArgs(head, second: nothing)))

	// The arena under the sweep and the bytes released so far. Every block below reaches both by DOMINANCE,
	// so only the two back edges carry them.
	let arena = ids.mint()
	let released = ids.mint()
	arenaLoop.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(arena, argType: StdType.i64))
	arenaLoop.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(released, argType: StdType.i64))

	let noArena = emitConst(module, block: arenaLoop, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let exhausted = emitCmp(module, block: arenaLoop, a: arena, b: noArena, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(arenaLoop, op: StdOp.condBranch(exhausted, thenBlockId: finish.id, elseBlockId: granuleStart.id))

	module.setTerminator(finish, op: StdOp.ret(released))

	let firstGranule = emitConst(module, block: granuleStart, value: 0, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(granuleStart, op: StdOp.branch(granuleLoop.id))
	granuleStart.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(granuleLoop.id, argIds: twoArgs(firstGranule, second: released)))

	let granule = ids.mint()
	let running = ids.mint()
	granuleLoop.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(granule, argType: StdType.i64))
	granuleLoop.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(running, argType: StdType.i64))

	let granuleLimit = emitConst(module, block: granuleLoop, value: geometry.granuleCount, ids: ids)
	let swept = emitCmp(module, block: granuleLoop, a: granule, b: granuleLimit, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(granuleLoop, op: StdOp.condBranch(swept, thenBlockId: nextArena.id, elseBlockId: testCommit.id))

	let following = emitLoadField(module, block: nextArena, record: arena, offset: ArenaNextOffset, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(nextArena, op: StdOp.branch(arenaLoop.id))
	nextArena.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(arenaLoop.id, argIds: twoArgs(following, second: running)))

	// The successor granule is computed ONCE here, where it dominates all three continuations, rather than at
	// each of them — three `+ 1`s over one loop variable is three chances for one of them to be wrong.
	let one = emitConst(module, block: testCommit, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let nextGranule = emitBin(module, block: testCommit, a: granule, b: one, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let commitWordAddr = emitBitmapWordAddr(module, block: testCommit, arenaBase: arena, bitIndex: granule, ids: ids)
	let commitWord = emitLoadField(module, block: testCommit, record: commitWordAddr, offset: geometry.commitBitmapOffset, ids: ids)
	let commitBitPos = emitBitPosition(module, block: testCommit, bitIndex: granule, ids: ids)
	let commitBit = emitSelectBit(module, block: testCommit, word: commitWord, bitPos: commitBitPos, ids: ids)
	let zero = emitConst(module, block: testCommit, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let notBacked = emitCmp(module, block: testCommit, a: commitBit, b: zero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(testCommit, op: StdOp.condBranch(notBacked, thenBlockId: skipUncommitted.id, elseBlockId: testFree.id))

	module.setTerminator(skipUncommitted, op: StdOp.branch(granuleLoop.id))
	skipUncommitted.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(granuleLoop.id, argIds: twoArgs(nextGranule, second: running)))

	// Is every chunk of this granule free? ONE masked read, because a granule's chunks are asserted to fit
	// inside one bitmap word (`checkSlabArenaGeometry`).
	let granuleChunksShift = emitConst(module, block: testFree, value: SlabArenaCommitGranuleChunksShift, ids: ids)
	let granuleFirstChunk = emitBin(module, block: testFree, a: granule, b: granuleChunksShift, opcode: StdBinOpcode.shl, ids: ids)
	let chunkWordAddr = emitBitmapWordAddr(module, block: testFree, arenaBase: arena, bitIndex: granuleFirstChunk, ids: ids)
	let chunkWord = emitLoadField(module, block: testFree, record: chunkWordAddr, offset: ArenaBitmapOffset, ids: ids)
	let chunkBitPos = emitBitPosition(module, block: testFree, bitIndex: granuleFirstChunk, ids: ids)
	let chunkField = emitSelectField(module, block: testFree, word: chunkWord, bitPos: chunkBitPos, width: SlabArenaCommitGranuleChunks, ids: ids)
	let allFreeField = emitConst(module, block: testFree, value: (1 shl SlabArenaCommitGranuleChunks) - 1, ids: ids)
	let inUse = emitCmp(module, block: testFree, a: chunkField, b: allFreeField, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(testFree, op: StdOp.condBranch(inUse, thenBlockId: skipInUse.id, elseBlockId: release.id))

	module.setTerminator(skipInUse, op: StdOp.branch(granuleLoop.id))
	skipInUse.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(granuleLoop.id, argIds: twoArgs(nextGranule, second: running)))

	let granuleShift = emitConst(module, block: release, value: SlabArenaCommitGranuleShift, ids: ids)
	let granuleOffset = emitBin(module, block: release, a: granule, b: granuleShift, opcode: StdBinOpcode.shl, ids: ids)
	let granuleBase = emitBin(module, block: release, a: arena, b: granuleOffset, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let granuleBytes = emitConst(module, block: release, value: SlabArenaCommitGranuleBytes, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(release, op: StdOp.osDecommitPages(granuleBase, sizeId: granuleBytes))

	// The bit falls only after the OS has been told, so a granule can never be marked unbacked while its pages
	// are still resident — the direction that matters, since the opposite marking is what a claim reads.
	// `not mask` is `mask xor -1`: the Std tier has no integer complement, and the all-ones constant is the
	// same answer (`emitBitRunUpdate` spells the identical trick).
	let commitMask = emitBitMask(module, block: release, bitPos: commitBitPos, ids: ids)
	let allOnes = emitConst(module, block: release, value: -1, ids: ids)
	let clearMask = emitBin(module, block: release, a: commitMask, b: allOnes, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitXor, ids: ids)
	let lowered = emitBin(module, block: release, a: commitWord, b: clearMask, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitAnd, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: release, record: commitWordAddr, offset: geometry.commitBitmapOffset, value: lowered)

	let grownTotal = emitBin(module, block: release, a: running, b: granuleBytes, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(release, op: StdOp.branch(granuleLoop.id))
	release.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(granuleLoop.id, argIds: twoArgs(nextGranule, second: grownTotal)))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSlabArenaScavenge'

// ⭐⭐ **WHICH DIRECTION A BIT RUN MOVES.** The allocate side and the release side are the SAME qword-and-bit
// arithmetic with opposite operators, and v1 writes the loop out twice — once inside
// `__slab_arena_alloc_chunks`'s `haveRun` branch and once as the whole of `__slab_arena_free_chunks`. Two
// copies of an index computation over one bitmap is exactly the pair that drifts: change the metadata
// reservation on one side and the two stop describing the same map.
//
// It is an enum rather than a bare `StdBinOpcode` parameter because the opcode is not the only thing that
// differs — the OPERAND's polarity does too (set a bit, or clear its complement) — and a caller handed two
// independent knobs can set them inconsistently. One name, both facts derived from it.
enum SlabBitRun
	// The chunks become USED: `word = word bitAnd not bit`.
	claim
	// The chunks become FREE: `word = word bitOr bit`.
	release
end 'SlabBitRun'

// Walk `[firstChunk, pastChunk)` and move each chunk's bit in `arenaBase`'s bitmap. Creates its own loop
// blocks off `cur` and answers the block execution continues in, so a caller threads it like any other
// loop-emitting builder in this file.
//
// ⭐ **IT TAKES THE RUN'S END, NOT ITS LENGTH, BECAUSE BOTH CALLERS ALREADY HOLD THE END.** `free_chunks`
// computes it for its range check and `alloc_chunks` for its commit watermark, so a `count` parameter would
// have made this function derive a third time what its callers had both derived already — three expressions
// that must agree about where a run stops.
//
// ⚠ A run may cross a bitmap-word boundary, which is why the word address is recomputed per bit rather than
// hoisted: a run of 64 chunks spans two words, and 10 pages of a large span can span two as well.
function emitBitRunUpdate(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, arenaBase ValueId, firstChunk ValueId, pastChunk ValueId, mode SlabBitRun, ids ValueMinter) returns IrBlock
	var header = module.addBlock(func, label: b"bitrunh")
	var body = module.addBlock(func, label: b"bitrunb")
	let done = module.addBlock(func, label: b"bitrund")

	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.branch(header.id))

	let chunk = ids.mint()
	header.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(chunk, argType: StdType.i64))
	cur.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(header.id, argIds: argsOf(firstChunk)))

	let atEnd = emitCmp(module, block: header, a: chunk, b: pastChunk, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(header, op: StdOp.condBranch(atEnd, thenBlockId: done.id, elseBlockId: body.id))

	let wordAddr = emitBitmapWordAddr(module, block: body, arenaBase: arenaBase, bitIndex: chunk, ids: ids)
	let word = emitLoadField(module, block: body, record: wordAddr, offset: ArenaBitmapOffset, ids: ids)
	let one = emitConst(module, block: body, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let bitPos = emitBitPosition(module, block: body, bitIndex: chunk, ids: ids)
	let bit = emitBitMask(module, block: body, bitPos: bitPos, ids: ids)

	// `not bit` as `bit xor -1`: the Std tier has no integer bitwise-complement opcode, and the all-ones
	// two's-complement constant is the same answer. v1 spells the same trick and says so.
	let operand = match mode 'polarity'
		claim gives emitBin(module, block: body, a: bit, b: emitConst(module, block: body, value: -1, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitXor, ids: ids)
		release gives bit
	end 'polarity'
	let opcode = match mode 'combine'
		claim gives StdBinOpcode.bitAnd
		release gives StdBinOpcode.bitOr
	end 'combine'

	let updated = emitBin(module, block: body, a: word, b: operand, opcode: opcode, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: body, record: wordAddr, offset: ArenaBitmapOffset, value: updated)

	let nextChunk = emitBin(module, block: body, a: chunk, b: one, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(body, op: StdOp.branch(header.id))
	body.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(header.id, argIds: argsOf(nextChunk)))

	return done
end 'emitBitRunUpdate'

// Back every commit granule the chunk run `[firstChunk, pastChunk)` touches that is not backed already, and
// record each one in `arenaBase`'s commit bitmap. Creates its own blocks off `cur` and answers the block
// execution continues in, exactly as `emitBitRunUpdate` does, so a caller threads it the same way.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE COMMIT BIT IS WHY A COMMIT CAN NEVER LAND ON A LIVE RANGE, AND THAT IS A CORRECTNESS ARGUMENT
// RATHER THAN AN OPTIMIZATION.** `StdOp.osCommitPages` DESTROYS the contents of the range it commits on the
// three POSIX lanes (it is an `mmap(MAP_FIXED)` there) and preserves them on Windows — so a re-commit of a
// range holding data is a silent data loss on three lanes and green on the one most likely to be tested. A
// granule whose bit is SET is skipped, and its bit is set exactly while its pages are backed: raised here
// once, cleared only by `__slab_arena_scavenge` and only for a granule whose every chunk is FREE. So the
// range committed here is always one whose contents nobody holds.
//
// ⛔ **IT REPLACED A WATERMARK, WHICH COULD NOT SURVIVE A SCAVENGER.** The old shape committed
// `[committedChunks, target)` — strictly above everything ever handed out, because the mark only rose — and
// that argument dies the moment something LOWERS the committed set in the middle. See `ArenaNextOffset`.
//
// ⭐ **THE UNIT IS THE GRANULE, NOT THE RUN.** Committing exactly the run would name ranges that are not
// page-aligned on arm64-macOS, whose page is 16 KiB — TWICE a chunk. `SlabArenaCommitGranuleBytes` says both
// why the number is what it is and why this is not a tuning knob; the same fact is what makes the
// scavenger's decommit legal on every lane.
function emitCommitGranulesFor(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, arenaBase ValueId, firstChunk ValueId, pastChunk ValueId, geometry SlabArenaGeometry, ids ValueMinter) returns IrBlock
	var header = module.addBlock(func, label: b"commith")
	var test = module.addBlock(func, label: b"committ")
	var commit = module.addBlock(func, label: b"commitc")
	var advance = module.addBlock(func, label: b"commita")
	let done = module.addBlock(func, label: b"committed")

	// The run's first and LAST granule — last rather than one-past, because `pastChunk` is one past the run
	// and `(pastChunk - 1) >> shift` is the granule the final chunk actually lives in. A one-past-the-end
	// granule index would commit a granule the run never reaches whenever the run ended on a boundary.
	let granuleChunksShift = emitConst(module, block: cur, value: SlabArenaCommitGranuleChunksShift, ids: ids)
	let firstGranule = emitBin(module, block: cur, a: firstChunk, b: granuleChunksShift, opcode: StdBinOpcode.shr, ids: ids)
	let one = emitConst(module, block: cur, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let lastChunk = emitBin(module, block: cur, a: pastChunk, b: one, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	let lastGranule = emitBin(module, block: cur, a: lastChunk, b: granuleChunksShift, opcode: StdBinOpcode.shr, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.branch(header.id))

	let granule = ids.mint()
	header.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(granule, argType: StdType.i64))
	cur.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(header.id, argIds: argsOf(firstGranule)))

	let past = emitCmp(module, block: header, a: granule, b: lastGranule, pred: StdCmpPred.gt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(header, op: StdOp.condBranch(past, thenBlockId: done.id, elseBlockId: test.id))

	let wordAddr = emitBitmapWordAddr(module, block: test, arenaBase: arenaBase, bitIndex: granule, ids: ids)
	let word = emitLoadField(module, block: test, record: wordAddr, offset: geometry.commitBitmapOffset, ids: ids)
	let bitPos = emitBitPosition(module, block: test, bitIndex: granule, ids: ids)
	let bit = emitSelectBit(module, block: test, word: word, bitPos: bitPos, ids: ids)
	let unbacked = emitConst(module, block: test, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let backed = emitCmp(module, block: test, a: bit, b: unbacked, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(test, op: StdOp.condBranch(backed, thenBlockId: advance.id, elseBlockId: commit.id))

	let granuleShift = emitConst(module, block: commit, value: SlabArenaCommitGranuleShift, ids: ids)
	let granuleOffset = emitBin(module, block: commit, a: granule, b: granuleShift, opcode: StdBinOpcode.shl, ids: ids)
	let granuleBase = emitBin(module, block: commit, a: arenaBase, b: granuleOffset, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let granuleBytes = emitConst(module, block: commit, value: SlabArenaCommitGranuleBytes, ids: ids)
	emitGuardedCommit(module, block: commit, base: granuleBase, byteCount: granuleBytes, ids: ids)

	// The bit is raised only after the OS has agreed, so a refused commit aborts inside the guard rather than
	// leaving the arena claiming pages it does not have.
	let setBit = emitBitMask(module, block: commit, bitPos: bitPos, ids: ids)
	let raised = emitBin(module, block: commit, a: word, b: setBit, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitOr, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: commit, record: wordAddr, offset: geometry.commitBitmapOffset, value: raised)
	module.setTerminator(commit, op: StdOp.branch(advance.id))

	let nextGranule = emitBin(module, block: advance, a: granule, b: one, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(advance, op: StdOp.branch(header.id))
	advance.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(header.id, argIds: argsOf(nextGranule)))

	return done
end 'emitCommitGranulesFor'

// Commit `byteCount` bytes at `base`, and abort cleanly if the OS refuses. Both of this file's commits go
// through it, so the check cannot be present at one site and missing at the other — which is the failure it
// exists to prevent, since an uncommitted page is INACCESSIBLE on Windows and on a `PROT_NONE` POSIX
// reservation alike, and the very next instruction at either site is a store.
//
// ⚠ The guard's ANSWER is its argument, and both callers already hold that address — what they want from it
// is its REFUSAL. Its result id is therefore minted inline at the call and never bound to a name, which is
// how this tier spells a discarded result (`SlabRuntime`'s `__slab_os_direct_free` call does the same); a
// named local nothing reads would read as an oversight rather than as a decision.
function emitGuardedCommit(module StdModule, block IrBlock, base ValueId, byteCount ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	let committed = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.osCommitPages(committed, addrId: base, sizeId: byteCount))
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: SlabAssertOsAllocName, args: argsOf(committed)))
end 'emitGuardedCommit'

// The address of the bitmap word holding `bitIndex`'s bit, MINUS the table's own offset — every caller loads
// and stores through that offset, so folding it into the addressing mode is one instruction saved per
// access and one place each bitmap's position is expressed.
//
// ⚠ **IT SERVES BOTH BITMAPS, AND THE TABLE IS THE CALLER'S TO NAME.** The chunk bitmap's bit index is a
// CHUNK and the commit bitmap's is a GRANULE; the word arithmetic is identical, and the only thing that
// differs is the offset the caller loads through (`ArenaBitmapOffset` or `geometry.commitBitmapOffset`).
// Writing the shift out a second time for the second table is exactly the drift `emitBitRunUpdate` exists
// to prevent one level up.
function emitBitmapWordAddr(module StdModule, block IrBlock, arenaBase ValueId, bitIndex ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let wordShift = emitConst(module, block: block, value: BitmapBitsPerWordShift, ids: ids)
	let wordIndex = emitBin(module, block: block, a: bitIndex, b: wordShift, opcode: StdBinOpcode.shr, ids: ids)
	return emitWordSlotAddr(module, block: block, base: arenaBase, index: wordIndex, ids: ids)
end 'emitBitmapWordAddr'

// `bitIndex` within its bitmap word — the twin of the address above, so the two halves of one index cannot
// be computed against different word widths.
function emitBitPosition(module StdModule, block IrBlock, bitIndex ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let mask = emitConst(module, block: block, value: BitmapBitsPerWordMask, ids: ids)
	return emitBin(module, block: block, a: bitIndex, b: mask, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitAnd, ids: ids)
end 'emitBitPosition'

// `(word shr bitPos) and 1` — one bit of a bitmap word, as 0 or 1. Three sites read a single bit (the
// first-fit scan, the commit test, the scavenger's commit test) and a fourth reads a MASKED FIELD of one
// (`emitSelectField`); spelled per site they are four places for the shift and the mask to disagree about
// which end of the word a bit lives at.
function emitSelectBit(module StdModule, block IrBlock, word ValueId, bitPos ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	return emitSelectField(module, block: block, word: word, bitPos: bitPos, width: 1, ids: ids)
end 'emitSelectBit'

// `(word shr bitPos) and ((1 shl width) - 1)` — `width` CONSECUTIVE bits of a bitmap word starting at
// `bitPos`. The scavenger's *"is every chunk of this granule free?"* is exactly this read against
// `SlabArenaCommitGranuleChunks` bits, which is why the single-bit form above is written as the width-1 case
// of it rather than beside it.
function emitSelectField(module StdModule, block IrBlock, word ValueId, bitPos ValueId, width ParsedInt, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let selected = emitBin(module, block: block, a: word, b: bitPos, opcode: StdBinOpcode.shr, ids: ids)
	let mask = emitConst(module, block: block, value: (1 shl width) - 1, ids: ids)
	return emitBin(module, block: block, a: selected, b: mask, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitAnd, ids: ids)
end 'emitSelectField'

// `1 shl bitPos` — the single-bit mask a set or a clear combines with. Its two callers set a commit bit and
// clear one, and a mask derived twice is the pair that ends up naming different bits.
function emitBitMask(module StdModule, block IrBlock, bitPos ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let one = emitConst(module, block: block, value: 1, ids: ids)
	return emitBin(module, block: block, a: one, b: bitPos, opcode: StdBinOpcode.shl, ids: ids)
end 'emitBitMask'

// `__slab_arena_map_ensure(addr) -> slotAddr` — the address of the reverse map's slot for the page holding
// `addr`, lazy-allocating whichever of the three levels do not exist yet.
//
// ⭐⭐ **IT ANSWERS THE SLOT, WHERE v1's ANSWERS NOTHING AND ITS CALLER RE-WALKS.** `__slab_arena_map_set`
// there runs `__slab_arena_map_ensure(pageAddr)` and then repeats the whole L1→L2→spans chain by hand to
// reach the slot the ensure had just been standing on — two walks, two copies of the index arithmetic, and
// two places for a shift to be wrong. Here the walk happens once and hands back where it ended.
//
// ⭐ **THE THREE LEVELS ARE ONE EMITTER CALLED THREE TIMES** (`emitLazyMapLevel`), which is what makes them
// three levels of one map rather than three near-identical blocks that must be edited together. The only
// per-level facts are the slot the pointer lives in and how many entries the table holds.
function buildSlabArenaMapEnsure(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SlabArenaMapEnsureName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	// The three merge blocks are created HERE rather than inside the emitter, because a caller that owns the
	// block can go on emitting into it — an emitter that CREATED it could only hand it back inside a pair
	// with the table base, and a block reached through an immutable field cannot be appended to (E3019).
	var atL1 = module.addBlock(func, label: b"l1")
	var atL2 = module.addBlock(func, label: b"l2")
	var atL3 = module.addBlock(func, label: b"l3")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let addr = 0 as ValueId

	let l1SlotAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.globalAddr(l1SlotAddr, label: SlabArenaMapL1Label))

	let l1Base = emitLazyMapLevel(module, func: func, cur: entry, slotAddr: l1SlotAddr, entryCount: ArenaMapL1Entries, have: atL1, ids: ids)
	let l2SlotAddr = emitMapSlotAddr(module, block: atL1, tableBase: l1Base, addr: addr, shift: ArenaMapL1Shift, mask: ArenaMapL1Mask, ids: ids)

	let l2Base = emitLazyMapLevel(module, func: func, cur: atL1, slotAddr: l2SlotAddr, entryCount: ArenaMapLevelEntries, have: atL2, ids: ids)
	let l3SlotAddr = emitMapSlotAddr(module, block: atL2, tableBase: l2Base, addr: addr, shift: ArenaMapL2Shift, mask: ArenaMapLevelMask, ids: ids)

	let l3Base = emitLazyMapLevel(module, func: func, cur: atL2, slotAddr: l3SlotAddr, entryCount: ArenaMapLevelEntries, have: atL3, ids: ids)
	let pageSlotAddr = emitMapSlotAddr(module, block: atL3, tableBase: l3Base, addr: addr, shift: SlabPageShift, mask: ArenaMapLevelMask, ids: ids)

	module.setTerminator(atL3, op: StdOp.ret(pageSlotAddr))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSlabArenaMapEnsure'

// Emit "load the table pointer at `slotAddr`; if it is 0, allocate and zero a table of `entryCount` words
// and publish it", merging both paths into `have`. Answers the ValueId of `have`'s block argument — the
// table's base address, which the caller then indexes.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THE ZEROING IS NOT OPTIONAL AND IT IS NOT PAID FOR BY THE OS.** A chunk from
// `__slab_arena_alloc_chunks` is fresh OS memory ONLY the first time it is handed out; the scan is first-fit
// over a bitmap, so a freed low chunk is reused before a virgin high one. An unzeroed level table reads a
// dead chunk's bytes as a table pointer, and the next `map_get` follows it. This is the same debt
// `MmRuntime`'s free-list note names — *"a slot returned to a free list holds the previous occupant's
// bytes"* — one layer down.
//
// ⚠ **FOLLOW-UP, NAMED: a per-arena virgin high-water mark.** ARCHITECTURE.md's zeroing contract describes
// exactly this at the SLOT level (`mspan.bump_next`: memory above the cursor has never been handed out and
// is therefore already zero). The chunk level has no such cursor yet, so every table is zeroed even when
// its chunk is provably untouched — a 512- or 8192-word loop run at most a handful of times per program.
function emitLazyMapLevel(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, slotAddr ValueId, entryCount ParsedInt, have IrBlock, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	var allocate = module.addBlock(func, label: mapLevelLabel(have.label, part: b"new"))
	var existing = module.addBlock(func, label: mapLevelLabel(have.label, part: b"had"))

	let current = emitLoadField(module, block: cur, record: slotAddr, offset: 0, ids: ids)
	let absent = emitConst(module, block: cur, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let missing = emitCmp(module, block: cur, a: current, b: absent, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.condBranch(missing, thenBlockId: allocate.id, elseBlockId: existing.id))

	module.setTerminator(existing, op: StdOp.branch(have.id))
	existing.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(have.id, argIds: argsOf(current)))

	let chunks = emitConst(module, block: allocate, value: arenaChunksFor(entryCount * ArenaWordBytes), ids: ids)
	let table = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(allocate, op: StdOp.call(table, callee: SlabArenaAllocChunksName, args: argsOf(chunks)))
	// ⭐ **ONE `memFill`, NOT A LOOP — AND THIS FILE'S PREMISE CHANGED UNDER IT MID-FLIGHT.** An earlier
	// draft zeroed the table with a hand-built Std-IR word loop, justified against the per-byte fill loop
	// then in the tree on the grounds that a word stride is eight times fewer iterations. **`StdOp.memFill`
	// landed in the same wave and retired both sides of that argument**: it is ONE op lowering to the
	// backend's size ladder (`rep stosq` on x64 at this size, a single `memory.fill` on wasm), so a 64 KiB
	// L2 table costs one instruction sequence rather than 8,192 loop iterations.
	//
	// The old builder is DELETED rather than left beside it. Two ways to zero a range is exactly the
	// duplication `emitBitRunUpdate` exists to prevent one level up, and a helper kept "in case" is how
	// the second one acquires a caller.
	let tableBytes = emitConst(module, block: allocate, value: entryCount * ArenaWordBytes, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(allocate, op: StdOp.memFill(table, byteCountId: tableBytes, value: 0))
	emitStoreField(module, block: allocate, record: slotAddr, offset: 0, value: table)
	module.setTerminator(allocate, op: StdOp.branch(have.id))
	allocate.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(have.id, argIds: argsOf(table)))

	let base = ids.mint()
	have.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(base, argType: StdType.i64))

	return base
end 'emitLazyMapLevel'

// One level's two private block labels — `newl2`, `hadl2`, derived from the merge block the caller named.
// Three calls of one emitter would otherwise put six identically-labelled blocks in
// `__slab_arena_map_ensure`; a label is not a block IDENTITY, so nothing breaks, but a disassembly of the
// one function that walks all three levels becomes unreadable.
function mapLevelLabel(name ByteArray, part ByteArray) returns ByteArray
	return "{String.from(part)}{String.from(name)}".toByteArray()
end 'mapLevelLabel'

// The address of one map slot: `tableBase + ((addr shr shift) and mask) * 8`. ONE spelling, shared by the
// SIX index computations — `ensure` indexes L1, L2 and L3, and `get` repeats all three — which is what
// makes "the L2 index is bits 26..38" a single statement rather than six. (The L1 TABLE's own address is a
// `globalAddr` in both, not an index, which is why there are six and not eight.)
function emitMapSlotAddr(module StdModule, block IrBlock, tableBase ValueId, addr ValueId, shift ParsedInt, mask ParsedInt, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let shiftConst = emitConst(module, block: block, value: shift, ids: ids)
	let raw = emitBin(module, block: block, a: addr, b: shiftConst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.shr, ids: ids)
	let maskConst = emitConst(module, block: block, value: mask, ids: ids)
	let index = emitBin(module, block: block, a: raw, b: maskConst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitAnd, ids: ids)
	return emitWordSlotAddr(module, block: block, base: tableBase, index: index, ids: ids)
end 'emitMapSlotAddr'

// `__slab_arena_map_set(base, numPages, span)` — register `span` as the owner of every page in
// `[base, base + numPages * SlabPageBytes)`.
//
// ⚠ **EVERY PAGE OF A MULTI-PAGE SPAN GETS ITS OWN SLOT, HOLDING THE SAME POINTER.** That is what makes
// `__slab_arena_map_get` answer for a pointer anywhere INSIDE the span rather than only at its base — which
// is the whole question a `free` asks.
function buildSlabArenaMapSet(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SlabArenaMapSetName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 3, paramTypes: threeI64Params(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(3)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var header = module.addBlock(func, label: b"pageh")
	var body = module.addBlock(func, label: b"pageb")
	var done = module.addBlock(func, label: b"paged")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 3)
	let base = 0 as ValueId
	let numPages = 1 as ValueId
	let span = 2 as ValueId

	let first = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 0, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.branch(header.id))

	let page = ids.mint()
	header.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(page, argType: StdType.i64))
	entry.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(header.id, argIds: argsOf(first)))

	let atEnd = emitCmp(module, block: header, a: page, b: numPages, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(header, op: StdOp.condBranch(atEnd, thenBlockId: done.id, elseBlockId: body.id))

	let pageShift = emitConst(module, block: body, value: SlabPageShift, ids: ids)
	let pageOffset = emitBin(module, block: body, a: page, b: pageShift, opcode: StdBinOpcode.shl, ids: ids)
	let pageAddr = emitBin(module, block: body, a: base, b: pageOffset, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let slot = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(body, op: StdOp.call(slot, callee: SlabArenaMapEnsureName, args: argsOf(pageAddr)))
	emitStoreField(module, block: body, record: slot, offset: 0, value: span)

	let one = emitConst(module, block: body, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let nextPage = emitBin(module, block: body, a: page, b: one, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(body, op: StdOp.branch(header.id))
	body.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(header.id, argIds: argsOf(nextPage)))

	module.setTerminator(done, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSlabArenaMapSet'

// **THE SPAN REGISTERED FOR THE PAGE HOLDING `ptr`, or `ArenaMapNoSpan`** — emitted INTO the caller's
// blocks, answering in `spanId`, the merge block's single argument.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE MISS IS AN ANSWER, NOT A DEFAULT.** Three levels can be absent, and every one of them means the
// same thing: nothing registered this address, so it did not come from a span — it came straight from the
// OS and the layer above releases it that way. `ArenaMapNoSpan` names that meaning so the three miss edges
// and the consumer cannot disagree about it. This is the one place in this file a zero return is not a
// sentinel standing in for a failure, and it is spelled out because it reads exactly like one.
//
// ⭐ **IT IS THE FIRST QUESTION EVERY FREE ASKS, AND SINCE EC8 `__slab_free` ASKS IT WITHOUT A CALL.** The
// walk is four loads and three tests over tables nothing ever overwrites — call-free by construction — so a
// frame around it was pure overhead on the path of every free in the language. `__slab_free_to_span`'s note
// makes the same argument for the push that follows it.
//
// It takes no lock and needs none even once the mutation paths take one: a published level pointer is never
// overwritten, so a reader either sees 0 or sees a fully zeroed table.
export function emitSlabArenaMapGet(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, ptr ValueId, spanId ValueId, ids ValueMinter, label ByteArray) returns IrBlock
	var atL2 = module.addBlock(func, label: label)
	var atL3 = module.addBlock(func, label: label)
	var atPage = module.addBlock(func, label: label)
	var miss = module.addBlock(func, label: label)
	var merge = module.addBlock(func, label: label)
	merge.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(spanId, argType: StdType.i64))

	let l1SlotAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(cur, op: StdOp.globalAddr(l1SlotAddr, label: SlabArenaMapL1Label))
	let l1 = emitLoadField(module, block: cur, record: l1SlotAddr, offset: 0, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnPresent(module, block: cur, pointer: l1, present: atL2, absent: miss, ids: ids)

	let l2SlotAddr = emitMapSlotAddr(module, block: atL2, tableBase: l1, addr: ptr, shift: ArenaMapL1Shift, mask: ArenaMapL1Mask, ids: ids)
	let l2 = emitLoadField(module, block: atL2, record: l2SlotAddr, offset: 0, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnPresent(module, block: atL2, pointer: l2, present: atL3, absent: miss, ids: ids)

	let l3SlotAddr = emitMapSlotAddr(module, block: atL3, tableBase: l2, addr: ptr, shift: ArenaMapL2Shift, mask: ArenaMapLevelMask, ids: ids)
	let l3 = emitLoadField(module, block: atL3, record: l3SlotAddr, offset: 0, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnPresent(module, block: atL3, pointer: l3, present: atPage, absent: miss, ids: ids)

	let pageSlotAddr = emitMapSlotAddr(module, block: atPage, tableBase: l3, addr: ptr, shift: SlabPageShift, mask: ArenaMapLevelMask, ids: ids)
	let span = emitLoadField(module, block: atPage, record: pageSlotAddr, offset: 0, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(atPage, op: StdOp.branch(merge.id))
	atPage.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(merge.id, argIds: argsOf(span)))

	let absent = emitConst(module, block: miss, value: ArenaMapNoSpan, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(miss, op: StdOp.branch(merge.id))
	miss.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(merge.id, argIds: argsOf(absent)))

	return merge
end 'emitSlabArenaMapGet'

// `__slab_arena_map_get(ptr) -> span` — the same walk behind a frame, for `__slab_drain_remote`, whose
// replay loop asks it once per queued slot. That loop is a MISS path, so a frame there costs a program
// nothing it would notice and saves the walk being written into the emitted image a second time.
function buildSlabArenaMapGet(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SlabArenaMapGetName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let ptr = 0 as ValueId

	let span = ids.mint()
	var answered = emitSlabArenaMapGet(module, func: func, cur: entry, ptr: ptr, spanId: span, ids: ids, label: b"map")
	module.setTerminator(answered, op: StdOp.ret(span))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSlabArenaMapGet'

// Continue into `present` when `pointer` is non-zero, else into `absent`. The lookup's three level tests are
// the same three ops, and writing them out per level is how one of the three ends up comparing the wrong
// value.
function emitBranchOnPresent(module StdModule, block IrBlock, pointer ValueId, present IrBlock, absent IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	let none = emitConst(module, block: block, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let uncommitted = emitCmp(module, block: block, a: pointer, b: none, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(block, op: StdOp.condBranch(uncommitted, thenBlockId: absent.id, elseBlockId: present.id))
end 'emitBranchOnPresent'
